const MOVIES_EXTRA = [{"num":577,"t":"Free Willy 2: The Adventure Home","y":"1995","la":"7+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Predictable sequel will thrill Willy fans; some peril.\" Concerns: kids are trapped on a sinking boat surrounded by flaming, oil-covered water during the climax, one boy falls from a rescue harness, and there's an awkward father-son talk after the dad hears rumors his tween has been kissing a girl he just met, plus one use of \"sucks.\" Positive: Jesse's devotion to Willy's welfare and marine conservation, his half-brother Elvis learning to drop his dishonesty and appreciate wildlife, and the blended family working through friction together with patience.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/free-willy-2-the-adventure-home","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":578,"t":"Free Willy","y":"1993","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"A wayward boy befriends a moody killer whale.\" Jesse, a street-tough delinquent, breaks into an aquarium and bonds with Willy the orca; the plot hinges on insurance fraud that may go over younger heads, and there's one brief fistfight plus a single use of \"screw.\" The climax involves trespassing and vandalism as Jesse's crew races to free Willy from captivity. Positive: shows how friendship and empathy can turn around a troubled kid's life, with strong messages about animal welfare.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/free-willy","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":579,"t":"Free Willy 3: The Rescue","y":"1997","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"For devoted -- and young -- Willy fans only.\" Jesse joins a whale-research expedition and discovers a fishing crew secretly hunting orcas, leading to threatened harpoonings and one barroom brawl, plus adult drunkenness in a few scenes. CSM notes the whalers aren't painted as cartoon villains but as men carrying on a hard-scrabble trade, even as the film pushes its conservation message hard. Positive: strong environmental and animal-welfare themes, though the slow, bedtime-story pacing best suits younger, dedicated fans.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/free-willy-3-the-rescue","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":580,"t":"Free Willy: Escape from Pirate's Cove","y":"2010","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Mild orca-tainment with adorable Bindi Irwin.\" Set in South Africa, the story centers on a captive orca threatened with poisoning; there are minor scuffles, a father hospitalized after a fall, a reference to a deceased mother, and adult beer drinking, though the language stays as mild as \"sack of scum.\" Positive: young heroine Kirra shows determination and moral conviction rallying her community to free the whale, carrying on the franchise's anti-captivity message.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/free-willy-escape-from-pirates-cove","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":581,"t":"Air Bud: Golden Receiver","y":"1998","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Cute but more intense than other Air Bud movies.\" Villains kidnap Buddy the golden retriever to force him into a circus act and keep other animals caged, football scenes get rough with no serious injuries, and mild slang like \"kick butt\" and \"fanny\" turns up alongside a widowed parent's new romance and a couple of kisses. Positive: models resilience and courage in coping with grief, and even shows the film's villains learning from their mistakes.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/air-bud-golden-receiver","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":582,"t":"Air Bud: World Pup","y":"2000","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Wholesome, endearing dog movie will delight grade schoolers.\" Buddy and Josh join the school soccer team, where a dog-napping subplot creates brief suspense (all puppies are safely returned), and the story folds in Josh's first crush plus a budding romance between Buddy and a new dog named Molly. Positive: CSM praises its unpreachy emphasis on teamwork, resisting peer pressure, and putting the team ahead of personal glory.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/air-bud-world-pup","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":583,"t":"Air Bud: Seventh Inning Fetch","y":"2002","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Terrific dogs but painfully slow, pointless sequel.\" A girl is struck by a fastball while catching, a car chase ends with a crash into a pigsty, and a scheming scientist wants to kidnap the dogs, though CSM flags the weak writing and thin plot more than anything genuinely troubling. Positive: heroine Andrea models practice-makes-perfect resilience and good sportsmanship as she works hard to improve at baseball.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/air-bud-seventh-inning-fetch","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":584,"t":"Air Bud: Spikes Back","y":"2003","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Cute dogs but dull plot; one of weaker Air Bud titles.\" Buddy is falsely accused of a museum jewel theft while escaped dogs cause chaos at a street fair, two bumbling thieves get thrown off a merry-go-round, and there's one notably gross vomit scene. Positive: Andrea earns her own money rather than asking her parents for it, modeling the value of saving and teamwork on the volleyball court.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/air-bud-spikes-back","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":585,"t":"Spider-Man","y":"2002","la":"10+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Fun superhero movie may be too intense for younger kids.\" Comic-book action gets intense: punching, strangling, and characters thrown through walls, plus a couple of graphic villain deaths. Mild profanity (one muffled harsher word, plus \"ass\" and \"damn\") and a couple of passionate kisses, including the famous upside-down rain kiss. Positive: Uncle Ben's \"with great power comes great responsibility\" anchors the whole film, and Peter Parker models empathy, self-control, and courage even when revenge tempts him.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":586,"t":"Spider-Man 2","y":"2004","la":"10+","ca":"11+","full":"\"A popcorn pleasure with heart, soul, and insight.\" Doc Ock's rampage brings cars hurled into buildings and people thrown from skyscrapers, including a genuinely upsetting scene where flying glass kills his wife. Language stays mild (just \"hell\"), and a heartbroken Harry is shown drinking whiskey alone. Positive: Peter's struggle to balance ordinary life with heroic duty models integrity and perseverance, and the film argues that people with real gifts shouldn't hide them just to fit in.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man-2","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":587,"t":"Spider-Man 3","y":"2007","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Kids will be dazzled, but the story falls short of No. 2.\" Extended battles bring visible blood, disfigurement, and character deaths, and the symbiote suit turns Peter aggressive enough that he strikes people he loves. Harry drinks heavily more than once, and there's mild language (\"damn,\" \"ass\"). Positive: the film centers on redemption, showing that people can walk back self-destructive choices, and even Sandman is drawn with sympathy as a father trying to help his sick daughter rather than a pure villain.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man-3","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":588,"t":"The Amazing Spider-Man","y":"2012","la":"11+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Action-packed series reboot has lots to offer Spidey fans.\" Comic-book violence runs heavy, with cars thrown off bridges, a bloody mugging victim, and a villain impaling a police officer with lizard claws. Language is occasional (\"ass,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\") and romance stays to flirting and a few kisses. Positive: \"with great power comes great responsibility\" drives the story again, and Gwen Stacy is written as smart and independent rather than a damsel, while Uncle Ben and Aunt May model steady, unconditional love.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-amazing-spider-man","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":589,"t":"The Amazing Spider-Man 2","y":"2014","la":"11+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Uneven Spider-Man sequel mixes action, romance.\" Large-scale destruction dominates the climax, with cop cars piling up, gunfire, and Manhattan landmarks toppling, plus a major character death that hits hard for a franchise film. Language stays mild (\"damn,\" \"hell,\" \"oh my God\"), and romance is limited to kisses and declarations of love between Peter and Gwen. Positive: the film's throughline is holding onto hope and passing it to others, with Peter shown as a devoted nephew, boyfriend, and friend valued for his generous spirit rather than his powers.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-amazing-spider-man-2","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":590,"t":"Spider-Man: Homecoming","y":"2017","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Diverse, entertaining reboot has some language, violence.\" Peter takes real hits, including getting buried in rubble and a genuinely tense ferry-splitting-in-half sequence, alongside regular but not constant profanity (\"s--t,\" \"bastard,\" \"damn\"). A house party has teens holding red cups and there's a brief drug reference and one crude joke. Positive: \"with great power comes great responsibility\" remains the core lesson, and Peter's eager, if impulsive, drive to help his diverse Queens community models courage, perseverance, and friendship.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man-homecoming","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":591,"t":"Spider-Man: Far From Home","y":"2019","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Fun, fast-paced sequel has some dark, dizzying violence.\" The Elemental battles bring mass destruction and dizzying, near-fall moments, plus mid-level language (\"bulls--t,\" a near-miss \"bitch,\" one partial harsher word). Peter appears shirtless a couple of times, and there's flirting and brief kissing with MJ. Positive: still grounded in \"with great power comes great responsibility,\" the film has Peter working through grief over Tony Stark's death while learning to balance personal wants against doing right by his community, with Ned's loyal friendship as a steady anchor.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man-far-from-home","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":592,"t":"Spider-Man: No Way Home","y":"2021","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Fun, funny, touching Spidey sequel has comic book violence.\" Multiverse battles bring characters slammed through walls and off high places, explosions, brief gunplay, and a major character's death that lands emotionally. Language includes an interrupted stronger word plus several uses of \"s--t\" and \"ass,\" and Aunt May catches a shirtless Peter with MJ. Positive: the film argues everyone deserves a second chance and that revenge doesn't actually fix anything, with Aunt May's compassion and Ned's loyalty reinforcing that great power still means great responsibility.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man-no-way-home","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":593,"t":"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse","y":"2023","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"\"Spidey sequel is an action-packed delight; peril, violence.\" Large-scale multiverse battles bring explosions, close-quarters fights, and characters held captive, with mild language like \"crap,\" \"ass,\" and \"hell.\" Miles and Gwen share clear romantic tension without any physical follow-through, and adults are shown drinking wine at a party. Positive: the film leans hard on courage, empathy, and mentorship, using Miles's fight against a rigid multiversal bureaucracy to explore imposter syndrome and gatekeeping, while Peter B. Parker models growth as a patient, present father figure.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man-across-the-spider-verse","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Sony Pictures Animation"},{"num":594,"t":"Hocus Pocus","y":"1993","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Rollicking Halloween fun has scares, gross-out moments,\" per CSM, which recommends this campy witch comedy for age 10+. The humor is broad, but there's a child's life-force drained early on, reanimated corpses, gallows imagery, gross-out bits like eating spiders, mild profanity (\"hell,\" \"damn\"), and innuendo built around virginity jokes; a couple of teens mention \"hash\" and cigarettes. Positive: Max, Dani, and Allison lean on teamwork and courage to outwit the witches, and the story shows bullying has real consequences.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/hocus-pocus","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":595,"t":"Now and Then","y":"1995","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"CSM calls this \"'90s coming-of-age movie has some sex, profanity,\" recommending it for age 12+ as four 12-year-old friends spend a formative summer together. Concerns include girls watching boys skinny-dip, frank talk about puberty and bodies, kids trying cigarettes, adult smoking, and mild language (\"s--t,\" \"bitch,\" \"hell\"); a storm-drain near-drowning and news accounts of a murder and fatal crash add real weight. Positive: the film argues that facing life's curveballs beats hiding from them, and the four leads model loyalty and empathy that carries their friendship into adulthood.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/now-and-then","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"New Line Cinema"},{"num":596,"t":"The Mighty Ducks","y":"1992","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Classic kids' hockey movie has iffy humor, language,\" says CSM, which sets the age bar at 10+. Coach Gordon Bombay starts the film freshly arrested for drinking and driving, and the ragtag team deals with body-checking, a player knocked unconscious against a goalpost, swearing (\"bastards,\" \"hell\"), and boys ogling a swimsuit magazine. Positive: Bombay learns that playing fair and having fun matter more than winning at all costs, while Charlie models maturity and devotion to his single mom.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-mighty-ducks","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":597,"t":"Beethoven","y":"1992","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"CSM's take: \"Lovable dog tale has violence, innuendo, slapstick,\" recommended for age 8+. Beyond the pratfalls, a veterinarian secretly runs a dog-fighting and animal-testing ring, there's a scary near-drowning, mild swearing (\"hell,\" \"damn\"), and a bedroom scene built around a husband-mistakes-the-dog-for-his-wife joke. Positive: the St. Bernard Beethoven is a steady, loyal presence who looks out for each of the kids, even as the human adults skew toward dimwits or villains.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/beethoven","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal"},{"num":598,"t":"3 Ninjas","y":"1992","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"\"1990s martial arts comedy; slapstick violence, potty humor,\" per CSM, age 9+. Kid heroes Rocky, Colt, and Tum Tum fight off adult kidnappers with nunchaku, swords, and plenty of groin-kick gags, plus toilet humor and mild insults. Positive: their grandfather's training stresses that martial arts are for defense, never for attacking the weak, and the brothers show real teamwork and courage under pressure.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/3-ninjas","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":599,"t":"Jumanji","y":"1995","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"CSM describes it as \"Effects-driven kids' adventure is charming but a bit grim,\" recommending age 10+. Once the cursed board game is opened, kids face stampeding animals, biting and stinging creatures, a hunter shooting at moving vehicles, and a boy getting beaten by bullies -- tense stuff even though language is limited to the occasional \"damn.\" Positive: Alan's arc is about breaking his own painful childhood by becoming the steady, encouraging adult he never had, and the film rewards facing fears instead of running from them.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jumanji","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"TriStar Pictures"},{"num":600,"t":"Mrs. Doubtfire","y":"1993","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Heartfelt divorce movie has language, outdated gender roles,\" says CSM, landing the recommended age at 13+. Robin Williams's Daniel disguises himself as a British nanny after a bitter divorce, and the film carries real profanity (including one f-word, plus \"s--t,\" \"bastard,\" \"hell\"), dinner-table innuendo about a parent's sex life, and moderate drinking and smoking. Positive: the kids end up being, in CSM's words, \"the anchor that pulls the best out of the people around them,\" and the story argues families can hold together in unconventional shapes even after separation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/mrs-doubtfire","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Drama"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":601,"t":"The Addams Family","y":"1991","la":"10+","ca":"11+","full":"CSM sums it up as a \"dark comedy has sexual innuendo, bloody images.\" Knives, swords, and other medieval weapons are visible throughout as the family jousts with real blades, ties each other to a torture rack, and lets the kids play with bows and an electric chair; two intruders get hurled from a window into open coffins. Gomez and Morticia's marriage is played as intensely flirtatious, with frequent kissing, sexual innuendo, and jokes about using torture devices for pleasure, plus mild language like \"damn\" and \"hell.\" Positive: Gomez and Morticia model a devoted, unshakeable partnership, and the family's real message is unconditional acceptance of anyone who doesn't fit in.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-addams-family-1991","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":602,"t":"Hook","y":"1991","la":"9+","ca":"10+","full":"CSM calls it a \"clumsy mishmash of a comedy has violence, profanity.\" Captain Hook kidnaps Peter Banning's kids and threatens to kill them, sword fights leave pirates dead on screen, and Hook fatally stabs a Lost Boy and slashes Peter's arm into a bloody gash; language includes \"ass,\" \"bitch,\" and \"fornicate,\" and pirates pass a brothel where Hook flirts with a sex worker. Adults drink whiskey and wine, and Peter himself gulps whiskey and stumbles. Positive: Peter's arc is genuinely redemptive, as an absent, workaholic father rediscovers play and puts his kids first, and the Lost Boys show what teamwork looks like even when it's chaotic.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/hook","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"TriStar Pictures"},{"num":603,"t":"Heavyweights","y":"1995","la":"8+","ca":"9+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Early Judd Apatow film makes fat camp mischievous and fun.\" A cruel new camp owner locks a struggling camper inside an electric-fenced cage as punishment and a father throws a punch, there's mild language like \"damn\" and \"kick his ass,\" and a bit of teen kissing at a dance. Positive: CSM flags real mixed messages here, some scenes push self-acceptance while others lean on cruelty and fat jokes toward the campers, so it's worth talking through with kids rather than taking at face value.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/heavyweights","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Sports"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":604,"t":"Mouse Hunt","y":"1997","la":"7+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Clever slapstick with cartoonish violence, profanity,\" per CSM. Two brothers wage all-out war on a single mouse using a shotgun, a nail gun, and eventually a house-leveling gas explosion, all played for over-the-top comedy; there's scatological gags, including the mouse defecating on an exterminator, lingerie-related innuendo, and language like \"bastard\" and \"son of a bitch.\" Positive: under all the cartoon mayhem, the story turns on the two feuding brothers reconciling and choosing family loyalty over their inheritance squabble.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/mouse-hunt","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"DreamWorks"},{"num":605,"t":"The Little Rascals","y":"1994","la":"7+","ca":"8+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Madcap, mischievous Our Gang remake has potty humor.\" The He-Man Woman Haters Club leans hard on \"girls are gross\" jokes and some anti-gay-coded humor, a bully threatens the gang, a Doberman knocks Alfalfa flat, and the clubhouse catches fire; there's potty humor and a few double entendres kids might pick up, like \"bite me.\" Positive: CSM notes the film offers little in the way of role models, though the racially integrated cast and the gang's loyalty to one another soften some of the dated attitudes carried over from the original.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-little-rascals-1994","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Universal"},{"num":606,"t":"The Parent Trap","y":"1998","la":"6+","ca":"7+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Delightful, family-friendly remake has smoking, drinking.\" One twin gets her ears pierced by her sister in a wincing DIY scene, a woman screams and tumbles into a lake over a small lizard, and there's real substance use: an anxious character smokes and drinks heavily, a vineyard-owning parent pours wine, and an 11-year-old is given a taste of wine at dinner. Positive: both divorced parents are shown as caring and devoted despite their split, and the twins are clever and resourceful, though CSM cautions that their scheming to reunite Mom and Dad plays more charming on screen than it would be if kids tried it themselves.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-parent-trap","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Romance"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":838,"t":"The Sheep Detectives","y":"2026","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Cozy family mystery with a murder at its center, so it is warmer than a crime premise sounds but still not for the youngest viewers. A beloved shepherd is found dead, characters grieve, a body is partly shown, and there are tense animal fights, aggressive dogs, talk of poison, a scary building, a crash aftermath, and adults shown with wine or whiskey. Language is infrequent but includes words like damn, crap, idiot, stupid, and shut up. Positive: Lily and the flock model courage, teamwork, perseverance, inclusion, and the idea that remembering loved ones keeps them close.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-sheep-detectives","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Mystery"],"studio":"Amazon MGM Studios"},{"num":839,"t":"Moana (2026)","y":"2026","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This live-action remake sticks close to the 2016 original's story, though the peril hits harder without animation's softening touch: a fiery lava monster, a violent storm at sea, coconut-shell pirates with weapons, and a glowing crab all return, plus a grandparent's death and return as a guiding spirit. Mild language includes \"son of a beach,\" \"sucks,\" \"dumb,\" and \"butt.\" Positive: Moana remains a strong, capable leader driven by her calling rather than romance, and Maui grows from selfishness into real mentorship; the film leans into courage, teamwork, communication, and perseverance, with authentic Pacific Islander casting throughout.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/moana-2026","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":840,"t":"Star Wars Episode V: The Empire Strikes Back","y":"1980","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"This first sequel darkens the saga's tone with laser battles, a Force-choking villain, a severed limb, and an off-screen torture scene, though profanity stays minimal. It still champions loyalty, courage, and sacrifice for others, and gives kids a tough, capable heroine in Leia.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-v-the-empire-strikes-back","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":841,"t":"Star Wars Episode VI: Return of the Jedi","y":"1983","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"The original trilogy's finale mixes lightsaber duels and a monster-pit sequence with a slow strangulation scene and a scantily-clad Leia in Jabba's palace. Themes of friendship and resisting a corrupt empire carry through, and Luke's self-control alongside Leia's boldness give kids strong examples to root for.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-vi-the-return-of-the-jedi","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":842,"t":"Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace","y":"1999","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"The first prequel keeps its violence mostly bloodless but includes a major character sliced apart by lightsaber, a tense podrace, and a heavy scene where a young boy is separated from his mother. It also explores how power can corrupt and how calm judgment matters, with Queen Amidala revealed as sharp and courageous underneath a passive exterior.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-i-the-phantom-menace","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":843,"t":"Star Wars Episode II: Attack of the Clones","y":"2002","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"This chapter ramps up battle violence with amputations, a poison-dart death, and a large arena fight, plus a bar scene where a dangerous offer of 'death sticks' is turned down. It keeps a clear line between right and wrong while showing a beloved hero start sliding toward darkness, alongside a gentle, mostly chaste romance.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-ii-attack-of-the-clones","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":844,"t":"Star Wars Episode III: Revenge of the Sith","y":"2005","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"The darkest prequel includes an off-screen massacre of children, a beheading, and a fiery amputation, making it the most intense entry for younger viewers. At its core it's a cautionary tale about anger and jealousy curdling into evil, with secondary characters modeling loyalty and integrity even as the hero falls.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-iii-revenge-of-the-sith","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":845,"t":"Star Wars Episode VII: The Force Awakens","y":"2015","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"This sequel-trilogy opener brings space battles, a torture scene, and an unsettling masked villain, plus a brief cantina moment implying drinking, though language stays mild. It leans into found-family themes and standing up for your values, introducing Rey and Finn as courageous, empathetic leads in a notably diverse cast.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-vii-the-force-awakens","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":846,"t":"Star Wars Episode VIII: The Last Jedi","y":"2017","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"Expect large-scale space combat, a bloodless in-half lightsaber moment, mild swearing like 'hell' and 'ass,' and a casino scene with drinks. The film keeps pushing courage, teamwork, and doing right even when it's hard, with several women holding key leadership roles across a diverse Resistance.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-viii-the-last-jedi","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":847,"t":"Star Wars Episode IX: The Rise of Skywalker","y":"2019","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"The saga's closer brings its heaviest action yet, including a beheading, an execution, and unsettling creature encounters, plus mild language and a drug-adjacent nickname for one character. It closes on messages of hope and redemption and the idea that people aren't fixed as purely good or bad, with Rey, Finn, and Poe modeling loyalty and teamwork.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-episode-ix-the-rise-of-skywalker","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":848,"t":"Rogue One: A Star Wars Story","y":"2016","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"This standalone war story is grimmer than most Star Wars films, with a high body count, civilians and a child in danger, and a mind-invasion torture sequence. It's built around sacrifice for a cause and doing right at great cost, headlined by Jyn Erso as a tough, capable lead in the franchise's most diverse cast yet.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/rogue-one-a-star-wars-story","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","War"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":849,"t":"Solo: A Star Wars Story","y":"2018","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"A younger Han Solo's origin story brings large battles, executions, a creature-confinement scare, pub drinking, and a bit more kissing than usual for this franchise, though language stays mild. It highlights teamwork and choosing to do right, while raising themes of equal treatment for droids and other beings in a cast with plenty of representation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/solo-a-star-wars-story","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":850,"t":"Star Wars: The Mandalorian and Grogu","y":"2026","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"This film brings blaster fights, scary monster encounters, and a poisonous bite, plus a cantina scene with light drinking and very mild language. Din Djarin's devotion to protecting Grogu underscores loyalty, responsibility, and found family, with heavy toy and merchandise tie-ins as the main non-content caveat.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-the-mandalorian-and-grogu","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":851,"t":"D2: The Mighty Ducks","y":"1994","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This hockey sequel has some rough, dirty on-ice hits that leave players hurt, plus very mild flirting and a couple of chaste kisses, with no swearing or substance use. Coach Bombay's arc about getting distracted by fame before refocusing on his players shows real growth, and the team wins through teamwork and knowing their own strengths.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/d2-the-mighty-ducks","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":852,"t":"D3: The Mighty Ducks","y":"1996","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"The trilogy closer has some rink roughhousing, a bit of comedic bullying, mild words like 'hell' and 'stupid,' and light teen romance. It pushes solid lessons about hard work, humility, and loyalty, including a storyline where kids push back on a demeaning team name, alongside a boys-and-girls-together roster.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/d3-the-mighty-ducks","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":853,"t":"Yellow Submarine","y":"1968","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This psychedelic Beatles feature keeps peril cartoonish and harmless, such as arrows that don't actually hurt anyone, though a group of villains smoke cigars and some songs carry rumored, never-depicted drug references. Otherwise it's a joyful, nonviolent celebration of creativity and wit, built entirely around a message of love overcoming division.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/yellow-submarine","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"United Artists"},{"num":854,"t":"Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets","y":"2002","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Harry's second year at Hogwarts brings genuinely scary content -- giant spiders, a deadly basilisk, and a house-elf who hurts himself as punishment -- plus one use of a wizarding slur. It's balanced by strong themes of friendship, courage, and teamwork, with Hermione's cleverness driving the plot.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-potter-and-the-chamber-of-secrets","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":855,"t":"Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban","y":"2004","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"Alfonso Cuaron's moodier take introduces the soul-sucking Dementors along with a werewolf chase and an injured hippogriff, plus mild slang like \"bloody\" and \"damn.\" Kids visit a pub and drink butterbeer. Positive throughlines include facing fear, loyalty, and compassion for misunderstood characters.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-potter-and-the-prisoner-of-azkaban","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":856,"t":"Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire","y":"2005","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"The Triwizard Tournament brings dragon attacks, an underwater rescue, and a genuinely upsetting on-screen death of a classmate, alongside first stirrings of teen romance and mild cursing. The series still centers on doing right under pressure, with Harry staying humble despite his sudden fame.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-potter-and-the-goblet-of-fire","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":857,"t":"Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix","y":"2007","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"Dolores Umbridge's cruel discipline, visions of Voldemort and the Death Eaters, and a major character's death make this a heavier entry, though the violence mostly stays bloodless. Students forming a secret defense club highlight courage and teamwork, and the story frames personal choices, not fate, as what defines someone.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-potter-and-the-order-of-the-phoenix","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":858,"t":"Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince","y":"2009","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"A beloved character is killed by a curse, and other scenes include poisoning, a life-threatening seizure, and Death Eaters setting fires, while teens drink butterbeer, mead, and wine at parties. Romantic subplots multiply alongside continued themes of loyalty and self-sacrifice, with Dumbledore held up as a selfless mentor.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-potter-and-the-half-blood-prince","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":859,"t":"Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows – Part 1","y":"2010","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"The franchise's darkest chapter yet has its highest body count, including a disturbing snake-from-corpse moment and a torture scene, alongside mild profanity. It stresses that heroism takes teamwork and that ancestry shouldn't determine worth, with ordinary people shown capable of real courage.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-potter-and-the-deathly-hallows-part-1","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":860,"t":"Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows – Part 2","y":"2011","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"The trilogy's climactic battle at Hogwarts brings extensive fighting, several major character deaths, and one gory creature-attack moment, plus a single strong swear word. It closes the series on themes of selflessness, loyal friendship, and unexpected bravery from side characters like Neville.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-potter-and-the-deathly-hallows-part-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":861,"t":"Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them","y":"2016","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"Set in 1920s New York, this more adult-leaning entry includes magical creature attacks, a belt-whipping that leaves visible marks, mild language, and a speakeasy scene implying alcohol. Newt Scamander models curiosity and compassion toward creatures others fear, and the film promotes understanding differences instead of assuming danger.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/fantastic-beasts-and-where-to-find-them","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":862,"t":"Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald","y":"2018","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"This sequel turns darker still, with magical battles, drownings, and a villain using deadly curses in an execution-style manner, alongside minimal profanity. It warns viewers to be wary of charismatic figures with hidden motives, while Newt and his allies model loyalty, hard work, and courage.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/fantastic-beasts-the-crimes-of-grindelwald","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":863,"t":"A Charlie Brown Christmas","y":"1965","la":"3+","ca":"3+","full":"This gentle holiday classic includes some name-calling of Charlie Brown by his friends and leans heavily into the Christian nativity story via Linus's reading. Ultimately the Peanuts gang rallies around Charlie Brown, delivering a message about Christmas mattering more for love and generosity than presents or commercialism.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/a-charlie-brown-christmas","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Holiday","Comedy"],"studio":"CBS"},{"num":864,"t":"How the Grinch Stole Christmas","y":"1966","la":"4+","ca":"4+","full":"Boris Karloff narrates this brief, largely gentle special whose roughest moment is the Grinch's dog being mistreated while pulling the overloaded sleigh. The story's core lesson -- that people can change and that the holiday is about love rather than gifts -- makes it a low-concern pick even for very young kids.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dr-seuss-how-the-grinch-stole-christmas","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Holiday","Comedy"],"studio":"MGM"},{"num":865,"t":"The Great Muppet Caper","y":"1981","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"This Muppet caper-comedy mixes slapstick tumbles, a bit of adult-aimed innuendo, and a passing curse word in a song lyric, plus a champagne toast. Kermit, Fozzie, and Gonzo solve the mystery through teamwork, and the film is notably body-positive toward Miss Piggy.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-great-muppet-caper","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":866,"t":"A Christmas Story","y":"1983","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Beneath its nostalgic tone, this comedy includes real bullying with a bloody-nosed fight, a frightening department-store Santa, several outdated and racially insensitive jokes, and language strong enough that a boy gets his mouth washed out with soap. It still lands on themes of self-control and family sticking together through a chaotic, imperfect holiday.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/a-christmas-story","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Holiday"],"studio":"MGM"},{"num":867,"t":"Nanny McPhee","y":"2005","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"Before order is restored, the unruly Brown children pull cartoonish pranks on a string of nannies, and the film includes potty humor, a mortuary scene with visible corpses, and one instance of British profanity. Nanny McPhee ultimately teaches the kids respect, cooperation, and care for one another through natural consequences rather than punishment.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/nanny-mcphee","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":868,"t":"Stuart Little 2","y":"2002","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This sequel has mild cartoon action -- chases, falls, and a menacing falcon -- plus a villain who meets a rough end, but nothing graphic; language is limited to light potty humor. The story rewards honesty and resilience, and Stuart's parents model warmth and trust throughout.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/stuart-little-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":869,"t":"Space Jam","y":"1996","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"This basketball comedy blends live action and Looney Tunes animation with nonstop slapstick violence (gunfire, explosions, falls), a few mild swears, a cigar-smoking villain, and heavy product placement, plus a joke that pokes fun at an overweight character. A flashback to a young Michael Jordan highlights hard work and dedication, and the team wins through cooperation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/space-jam","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Animation","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":870,"t":"The Chronicles of Narnia: The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe","y":"2005","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This fantasy adaptation carries battle sequences and a genuinely frightening White Witch that could unsettle younger or sensitive kids. The story carries overt religious allegory about good triumphing over evil, which families may want to discuss together. There's no language, drug use, or sexual content to worry about.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-chronicles-of-narnia-the-lion-the-witch-the-wardrobe","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"Disney"},{"num":872,"t":"Willow","y":"1988","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"This Lucasfilm-produced fantasy adventure has sword fights, scary villains, a two-headed monster, and a tavern brawl with drunken behavior, plus some crude innuendo and mild slurs. Despite the peril, the film champions courage, loyalty, and sacrifice, and gives its short-statured hero a fully realized, non-tokenized starring role.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/willow","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":874,"t":"The Lego Ninjago Movie","y":"2017","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This entry has cartoonish martial-arts action (including a limb loss played for laughs) and virtually no bad language. It leans on messages of self-acceptance, forgiveness, and reconciling with an estranged parent, alongside a diverse voice cast, though it's even more overtly a marketing vehicle for the toy line.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-lego-ninjago-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":875,"t":"The Lego Movie 2: The Second Part","y":"2019","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This sequel has Mad-Max-style action, a monstrous Unikitty transformation, and mild words like 'jerk,' plus a wink to an adult movie franchise. Its throughline is about accepting friends as they are and staying loyal under pressure, modeled by an unfailingly upbeat Emmet.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-lego-movie-2-the-second-part","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":876,"t":"Who Framed Roger Rabbit","y":"1988","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"This noir hybrid's cartoon-violence gags get dark (an acid-like substance that erases characters), the detective hero is a heavy daytime drinker, Jessica Rabbit is drawn very provocatively, and the human characters swear more than expected for a PG movie. Its core message -- not to prejudge people, or toons, by appearances -- lands well for older kids who can handle the tone.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/who-framed-roger-rabbit","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Animation","Comedy"],"studio":"Touchstone Pictures"},{"num":878,"t":"The Secret of NIMH","y":"1982","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This Don Bluth-directed animated film swings dark for a G-era rating -- a rat dies in a sword duel, animals are shown being experimented on and injected, and a giant spider appears before being crushed. It ultimately celebrates a mother's courage and teamwork in protecting her family, but younger kids may find the imagery genuinely creepy.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-secret-of-nimh","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"United Artists"},{"num":879,"t":"The Transformers: The Movie","y":"1986","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This animated sci-fi actioner has near-constant robot destruction, some incidental swearing, and the death of several likable characters -- unusually dark for what's essentially an extended toy commercial. It still promotes courage, loyalty, and self-belief among the Autobots, though the story leans on the idea that war is unavoidable.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-transformers-the-movie-1986","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Action","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"De Laurentiis Entertainment Group"},{"num":881,"t":"Jurassic Park","y":"1993","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Spielberg's original pits terrifyingly lifelike dinosaurs against a small group of park visitors, with several people mauled or eaten and one gruesome severed-limb moment. There's occasional strong language, background smoking and drinking, and sustained danger for two child characters. On the upside, it rewards teamwork, quick thinking, and courage, and its female leads come across as sharp, capable problem-solvers.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jurassic-park","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":882,"t":"The Lost World: Jurassic Park","y":"1997","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"This sequel turns up the gore, including one especially brutal death, as dinosaurs defending their young go on the attack, first on a remote island and then loose in Los Angeles. Expect scattered strong language and light drinking, plus a subplot involving cruelty to a baby dinosaur. It leans into a message about the fallout of human meddling with nature, and its female scientist proves just as resourceful as the men around her.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-lost-world-jurassic-park","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":883,"t":"Jurassic Park III","y":"2001","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"A shorter, faster-paced entry that keeps its characters in near-constant danger from a new, more aggressive dinosaur, with a few side characters killed off along the way. Content is fairly mild otherwise: a little language, adults drinking at a bar, and a brief glimpse of a woman in her underwear. A child character shows real grit under pressure, and the story quietly warns against pushing too far into nature's territory.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jurassic-park-iii","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":884,"t":"Jurassic World","y":"2015","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"The franchise reboot amps up the scale and body count, as a genetically engineered super-predator escapes a fully operational theme park and kills numerous people over the course of the film. There's moderate swearing, heavy tension, and kids placed in real danger, plus a lot of visible brand tie-ins. It still lands on themes of courage, compassion, and teamwork, and reminds viewers that nature can't simply be engineered into submission.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jurassic-world","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":885,"t":"Jurassic World: Fallen Kingdom","y":"2018","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"Set on an erupting volcanic island, this sequel delivers relentless peril and a genuinely frightening stretch where an engineered dinosaur specifically hunts a young girl; deaths include burning, trampling, and worse. Language stays fairly light and substance use is minimal, though product placement is everywhere. It keeps the series' running theme that science shouldn't outrun ethics, with its leads showing real bravery and compassion for the animals in their care.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jurassic-world-fallen-kingdom","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":886,"t":"Jurassic World Dominion","y":"2022","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"The trilogy-closer mixes dinosaur attacks with human-on-human fights and a swarm of oversized, kid-terrorizing locusts, and it lands a handful of mild profanities along the way. Children are put in danger more than once, though the returning original trio (Sattler, Grant, Malcolm) anchor the film's push for integrity over corporate profit. Overall it favors teamwork and doing the right thing, even as the action stays intense.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jurassic-world-dominion","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":887,"t":"Jurassic World Rebirth","y":"2025","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"A mercenary crew hunting dinosaur DNA runs into near-constant attacks, with some fairly graphic imagery (a severed arm, skeletal remains) and a couple of gut-punch fake-out scares involving kids. There's infrequent strong language, some drinking, and one throwaway joke about marijuana made to a tween. Its emotional core is redemption -- self-interested characters learn to act selflessly -- alongside themes of teamwork and putting people ahead of profit.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jurassic-world-rebirth","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":888,"t":"The Hobbit","y":"1977","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"This Rankin/Bass animated TV special condenses Tolkien's story into a family-friendly adventure with sword fights, a fire-breathing dragon, giant spiders, and menacing trolls; a few dead bodies are shown at a distance after the climactic battle. Pipe-smoking is a running visual gag, and the wood-elves get tipsy on wine at one point, but there's no language or romance to worry about. It's ultimately a gentle lesson in how an unassuming, comfort-loving hero can grow into someone genuinely brave, and how greed and warfare accomplish little.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-hobbit-1977","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Rankin/Bass Productions"},{"num":889,"t":"The End of Oak Street","y":"2026","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"A 1980s suburban cul-de-sac gets yanked into dinosaur-infested territory in this sci-fi thriller, and it earns its higher age rating with bloody corpses, severed body parts, and creatures that snatch and devour people on camera. There's a bit of strong language and an adult secretly drinking to cope with the crisis. Still, the story centers a struggling couple who ultimately put their kids' safety first, framing courage as something that can look like calm, positive thinking rather than physical heroics.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-end-of-oak-street","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Horror"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":891,"t":"The Railway Children","y":"1970","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"Three siblings relocate to the countryside after their father mysteriously vanishes, and they end up risking their own safety to stop a train wreck. It's a gentle period piece with only mild trouble spots -- a slap, a bloody broken leg, a doctor's hip flask, and a railway worker having a beer. The children come across as responsible, kind, and resourceful, and the story rewards good manners and looking out for others.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-railway-children","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Adventure"],"studio":"EMI Films"},{"num":892,"t":"The Water Horse: Legend of the Deep","y":"2007","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"A lonely Scottish boy hatches a mysterious egg into a fast-growing lake creature and has to keep it hidden from soldiers who eventually shoot at and bombard it with artillery; a family dog is also killed off-screen. There's some social drinking and smoking at a wartime banquet and pub, plus mild language, but nothing stronger. The soldiers are cast as bullies against the children's compassion for their misunderstood pet, and the film leans on themes of loyalty and quiet cooperation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-water-horse-legend-of-the-deep","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":894,"t":"Nacho Libre","y":"2006","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"A monastery cook moonlights as a masked wrestler to earn money for the orphans in his care, with plenty of slapstick ring violence, food gags, and heavy bathroom humor along the way. Mild profanity and a broadly played accent are present, but the story is ultimately driven by generosity and helping people in need.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/nacho-libre","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Sports"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":895,"t":"Tron","y":"1982","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"A programmer gets pulled inside a computer world where he must battle a corrupt system in stylized, video-game-style action, including one unsettling scene with a glowing skeleton. Mild language and vague references to past relationships appear, but the story functions as a light allegory about standing up to corruption, with a scientist heroine among the leads.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/tron","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Action"],"studio":"Walt Disney Productions"},{"num":896,"t":"Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade","y":"1989","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Indy and his estranged father race Nazis across Europe to find the Holy Grail, with frequent hand-to-hand fighting, gunplay, and a couple of gruesome side-character deaths. There's mild language and some suggestive banter, but the film centers on courage, cleverness, and repairing a strained father-son bond, despite some dated ethnic stereotyping in its depiction of the region.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/indiana-jones-and-the-last-crusade","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Action"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":897,"t":"Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom","y":"1984","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Indy, a nightclub singer, and a young sidekick square off against a murderous cult, including a notoriously intense ritual sacrifice scene and other nightmare-inducing imagery. There's whipping, hand-to-hand combat, and gross-out food moments, alongside mild profanity, but the film also rewards courage, loyalty, and teamwork between its unlikely trio, despite some dated depictions of Indian and Asian characters.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/indiana-jones-and-the-temple-of-doom","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Action"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":898,"t":"Indiana Jones and the Kingdom of the Crystal Skull","y":"2008","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Now facing Soviet agents in the 1950s, Indy chases a mysterious crystal skull through jungle traps, a killer-ant attack, and an atomic-test sequence that could unsettle younger viewers. Fights involve guns, swords, and some blood, with mild language throughout, but the film keeps its focus on ingenuity, loyalty, and protecting history from those who'd exploit it.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/indiana-jones-and-the-kingdom-of-the-crystal-skull","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Action"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":899,"t":"Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny","y":"2023","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"In his final outing, an aging Indy chases a time-bending artifact while enemies shoot bystanders abruptly and sometimes execution-style, making this entry noticeably darker than earlier films. Frequent drinking and smoking appear alongside mild language, but the story leans heavily on themes of found family and people who show up for one another.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/indiana-jones-and-the-dial-of-destiny","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Action"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":901,"t":"Labyrinth","y":"1986","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A teenage girl must navigate a goblin king's magical maze to rescue her baby brother, encountering puppet creatures, sword fights, and a villain whose fixation on her has an uncomfortable undertone for adult viewers to notice. There's no real gore and only mild exclamations for language, and the story rewards Sarah's cleverness, courage, and willingness to work with unlikely allies.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/labyrinth","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"Tri-Star Pictures"},{"num":902,"t":"Ghostbusters","y":"1984","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"A trio of scientists-turned-paranormal-exterminators battle decomposing ghosts and a possessed woman while blasting energy weapons around New York. Swearing is fairly frequent and one lead's persistent pursuit of a woman who keeps turning him down plays as comedy in a way that hasn't aged well, though the film's central idea of scientists saving the day through teamwork and curiosity still lands.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ghostbusters","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":903,"t":"Ghostbusters II","y":"1989","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"The team reunites to stop a river of slime and demonic imagery, including a startling shot of severed heads and some real peril involving a baby near traffic and a high ledge. Language stays infrequent and mild, and while the characters are too cartoonish to be true role models, the story's message about kindness overcoming negativity comes through as goofy family entertainment.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ghostbusters-ii","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":904,"t":"The Karate Kid Part II","y":"1986","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Daniel and Mr. Miyagi travel to Okinawa, where old rivalries reignite into severe karate beatings and threatening confrontations, plus some gambling on a fight's outcome. The film preaches mercy and forgiveness even as its climax resolves through combat, but the cross-cultural mentorship between Daniel and Miyagi still offers genuinely touching moments.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-karate-kid-part-ii","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Action"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":905,"t":"The Karate Kid Part III","y":"1989","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"A revenge-driven new villain manipulates Daniel's training in this weaker sequel, which includes a bloody-knuckled fight scene, a tense cliffside confrontation, and a racial slur aimed at an Asian character. The antagonist smokes and drinks, and mild profanity recurs throughout, but Mr. Miyagi still models integrity, teaching that self-belief and hard work outlast brute strength and grudges.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-karate-kid-part-iii","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Action"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":906,"t":"Gremlins","y":"1984","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"This horror-comedy gets surprisingly graphic for a PG-era movie, with a blender death, a shooting, an explosion, and a character thrown from a window, plus scattered swearing and references to suicide. Gremlins and humans alike are shown smoking and drinking, and some transformation and jump-scare sequences are genuinely unsettling for younger kids. The teen leads show real courage and resourcefulness fighting back against the chaos, and the story doubles as a cautionary tale about greed and messing with things you don't understand.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/gremlins","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Horror"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":907,"t":"Gremlins 2: The New Batch","y":"1990","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"The sequel leans hard into slapstick, with gremlins meeting cartoonish but graphic ends (including one shredded on-screen) and plenty of drinking and smoking played for laughs. Language stays mild, limited mostly to \"hell,\" and the tone is more goofy satire of corporate greed and pop culture than genuine horror. There's little real substance or role-modeling here beyond Gizmo's small moments of heroism, so it's best treated as chaotic fun rather than a movie with a lesson.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/gremlins-2-the-new-batch","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Horror"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":908,"t":"The Santa Clause 2","y":"2002","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"A gentle family comedy with a bit of slapstick and some bathroom humor, including a robot Santa double whose rear end gets an unnecessary close-up and who acts tipsy after drinking cocoa. There's noticeable brand placement for fast food and candy, and the elf world leans on old-fashioned gender roles. It does touch on the real meaning of Christmas, though the film ultimately cares more about presents than heart, even with some capable female characters in the mix.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-santa-clause-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Holiday"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":909,"t":"The Santa Clause 3: The Escape Clause","y":"2006","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"This lighter, sillier entry keeps things G-rated, with only mild slapstick like a shovel to the face or a pratfall off a roof for danger. There's no bad language or scary content to speak of. The story sets up a clear contrast between Scott's genuine holiday spirit and Jack Frost's selfish scheme to steal Christmas for himself, giving families an easy opening to talk about generosity versus greed.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-santa-clause-3-the-escape-clause","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Holiday"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":910,"t":"Turner & Hooch","y":"1989","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"This buddy-cop comedy opens with a stabbing and includes a dog being shot and dying on screen, along with mild cursing and a drug-smuggling subplot, so it's heavier than its goofy dog-movie premise suggests. Hooch himself drinks beer for laughs in a couple of scenes. Underneath the mess and noise, the film rewards Turner's persistence and integrity, offering a message about loyalty and following through even when a case, or a partnership, seems hopeless.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/turner-hooch","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Buena Vista Pictures"},{"num":911,"t":"Flash Gordon","y":"1980","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"This campy space opera stages its violence in an unrealistic, comic-book way, ray-gun blasts, impalements, and bodies dissolving, but a few images (like an eye being gouged from a cyborg) are still fairly gruesome for a PG movie. Swearing is minor, an alien drink and a passing pill reference cover substance use, and skimpy costumes plus some ogling account for the mild sexual content. Underneath the spectacle, the story champions the idea that ordinary courage can unite the oppressed against a tyrant, even if the characters themselves stay pretty broad and archetypal.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/flash-gordon-1980","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":912,"t":"The Muppets Take Manhattan","y":"1984","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"A mostly gentle backstage comedy where the roughest moments are Kermit getting hit by a car (leading to amnesia) and Miss Piggy fending off a mugger and some construction-worker leering, plus a bit of cigarette smoking in the background. The jokes work on two levels, broad slapstick for kids and knowing references for adults, so younger viewers won't catch everything but won't be bothered by it either. The heart of the film is Kermit and company's determination to make it on Broadway despite repeated rejection, modeling persistence and teamwork.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-muppets-take-manhattan","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"TriStar Pictures"},{"num":913,"t":"Harry and the Hendersons","y":"1987","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"The biggest surprise for parents here is the language, several instances of stronger swearing than you'd expect from a family Bigfoot comedy, while the action itself stays cartoonish with no blood despite guns and hunting scenes. Some of the hunter sequences may be intense for younger or more sensitive kids. The film's real strength is its message of empathy: the Henderson father starts out as a hunter and, with his family's help, comes to protect Harry instead of harming him.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/harry-and-the-hendersons","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":914,"t":"Popeye","y":"1980","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Robert Altman's odd, musical take on the comic strip keeps its fights with Bluto and other villains cartoonish and harmless, with only a few mild curse words scattered through the dialogue. There's nothing to flag around scares, substances, or sexual content. It's a quirky, love-it-or-hate-it film, but Popeye's willingness to take in an abandoned baby and look out for his community comes through as a quiet, caring throughline.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/popeye","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":915,"t":"All Dogs Go to Heaven","y":"1989","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This Don Bluth musical is darker than its cute premise suggests: the dog characters are basically Prohibition-era gangsters who drink at a bar, smoke cigars (even blowing smoke at a child), gamble, steal, and try to kill one another, and there's a nightmarish, demon-tinged sequence plus a brief racial caricature. Despite all that, the throughline is redemption -- Charlie's arc is about learning that loyalty and friendship matter more than revenge or self-interest, and that people (and dogs) can change for the better.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/all-dogs-go-to-heaven","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"MGM/UA"},{"num":916,"t":"The Jetsons Meet the Flintstones","y":"1987","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. This Hanna-Barbera TV crossover sends the Jetsons back to the Stone Age (and the Flintstones forward to the Space Age) through a time-machine mix-up, playing out as light workplace and family comedy rather than anything scary or edgy. No violence, language, or substance content is flagged, consistent with its made-for-syndication, all-ages design. The story mostly celebrates friendship and loyalty, as Fred and Barney's bond is tested and repaired, and both families end up helping each other out of job trouble.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Jetsons_Meet_the_Flintstones","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Hanna-Barbera Productions"},{"num":917,"t":"Mickey's Christmas Carol","y":"1983","la":"4+","ca":"4+","full":"This short, Disney-fied Dickens adaptation softens the story for young viewers but still has a few mildly frightening beats, a menacing Ghost of Christmas Future and a burning coffin, plus background cigar and cigarette smoking. In one odd moment Mickey roughly grabs Pluto by the neck, which sits oddly against his usual kindly persona. Overall it delivers the classic lesson that greed isolates and harms people while generosity brings connection and joy, using Scrooge's turnaround as the emotional core.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/disney-animation-collection-7-mickeys-christmas-carol","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Holiday","Fantasy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Productions"},{"num":918,"t":"My Little Pony: The Movie","y":"1986","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"A toyline tie-in aimed squarely at young kids, with cartoon peril, a menacing purple ooze, a spider-like monster, and glowing-eyed trees, plus a trio of cruel witches who shove and strike each other, that could startle the youngest viewers even though nothing is graphic. Insults among the witches (like \"stupid\" and \"pea brain\") are as edgy as the language gets, and there's no substance use or sexual content at all. The ponies model bravery, teamwork, and facing problems head-on rather than running away.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/my-little-pony-the-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"De Laurentiis Entertainment Group"},{"num":919,"t":"The Wiz","y":"1978","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This musical reimagining of The Wizard of Oz features an all-Black cast including Diana Ross and Michael Jackson, with fantasy peril like flying monkeys, a fire-breathing giant head, and scenes where characters are threatened or harmed. Adults drink and smoke at a holiday dinner, there's some mild language, and one storyline touches on a suicide attempt that's stopped by friends. Positive themes of friendship, resilience, and finding inner strength run throughout as the characters support each other on their journey.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-wiz","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":920,"t":"Little Monsters","y":"1989","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. This fantasy comedy follows a sixth-grader who befriends a monster living under his bed and discovers a hidden underground world where monsters sneak into kids' rooms at night to cause mischief. Expect plenty of gross-out gags and slapstick along with a few genuinely spooky moments involving a menacing villain, though the tone stays playful and kid-friendly overall. At its core it's a story about an unlikely friendship and standing up for someone in trouble.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Monsters_(1989_film)","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"United Artists"},{"num":921,"t":"Richie Rich","y":"1994","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"Macaulay Culkin stars as the world's richest kid in this gadget-filled comedy that leans heavily on flashy toys and product placement. There's some cartoonish action-movie violence with guns and lasers used at close range plus a tense plane sequence, along with a bit of mild language and a brief suggestive workout scene. Underneath the consumerism, the film argues that money can't buy real friendship and that treating employees and friends with genuine kindness matters more than wealth.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/richie-rich","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":922,"t":"It Takes Two","y":"1995","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"The Olsen twins play lookalike strangers, an orphan and a wealthy heiress, who swap places to bring a caring social worker and a wealthy dad together. The plot leans on the girls sneaking around, lying to adults, and even taking a horse without permission, plus some mild name-calling and a jilted bride who threatens to strike the kids. It's a predictable but harmless comedy that wraps up happily, though it doesn't offer much in the way of explicit positive lessons.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/it-takes-two","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Romance"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":923,"t":"Babes in Toyland","y":"1986","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. In this NBC television musical, a young Drew Barrymore is whisked away to a magical Toyland on Christmas Eve, teaming up with a hero played by Keanu Reeves to stop a villain from taking over. The story includes a menacing antagonist and a monster that threatens the young heroes, presented within a family Christmas musical framework. Themes of courage, friendship, and Christmas spirit anchor the fantasy adventure.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babes_in_Toyland_(1986_film)","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"Orion Television"},{"num":924,"t":"Babes in Toyland","y":"1997","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. This direct-to-video animated musical sends orphaned siblings Jack and Jill to live with their villainous Uncle Barnaby in the land of Toyland, where goblins led by a menacing Goblin King pursue and try to imprison the children. Expect some frightening imagery, including a character being knocked over a bridge and scenes of goblins invading and setting fires, though nothing especially graphic. The film sticks to themes of family loyalty and courage typical of the source operetta.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babes_in_Toyland_(1997_film)","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer"},{"num":925,"t":"The Care Bears Movie","y":"1985","la":"4+","ca":"4+","full":"The original Care Bears film follows two grieving orphaned children and a lonely boy who falls under the influence of a shadowy Evil Spirit threatening to end all caring in the world. Preschoolers may find the misty villain and scenes of destruction a little intense, though there's no language or substance use to worry about. The film's heart is squarely on teamwork, compassion, and banding together against negativity.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-care-bears-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"The Samuel Goldwyn Company"},{"num":926,"t":"Care Bears Movie II: A New Generation","y":"1986","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This origin story for the Care Bears finds baby bear cubs kidnapped and caged by the villain Dark Heart, with one tense scene where a girl is accidentally zapped into another dimension. There's no bad language or substance use, but the film is fairly thin on educational value and heavy on toy tie-ins. What positive material there is centers on teamwork, guardians protecting their young, and gently addressing bullying.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-care-bears-movie-ii-a-new-generation","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":927,"t":"The Adventures of Elmo in Grouchland","y":"1999","la":"3+","ca":"3+","full":"Elmo chases his beloved blanket into Grouchland, a topsy-turvy world where being rude is the norm, and must get it back from a selfish villain who hoards everything he touches. A few perilous moments, like a rollerblading chase and a fall down a tunnel, might startle very sensitive toddlers, but there's essentially no scary language or violence. The film's heart is all about sharing, cooperation, and believing in yourself, wrapped in classic Sesame Street songs and humor.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-adventures-of-elmo-in-grouchland","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":928,"t":"The Brave Little Toaster to the Rescue","y":"1997","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"In this sequel, the appliances head off to rescue their owner's college thesis from a computer virus and to save some animal friends from being sold to a testing lab. The peril stays mild and cartoonish, with only brief, non-graphic references to animals being mistreated and a little name-calling. The film's real focus is on friendship, loyalty, and not discarding things, or people, just because they're no longer new.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-brave-little-toaster-to-the-rescue","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Walt Disney Home Video"},{"num":929,"t":"Casper","y":"1995","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Christina Ricci plays a grieving teen who befriends a friendly ghost haunting the mansion her father is hired to exorcise, in a film built around groundbreaking CGI for its time. There's a fair amount of ghostly slapstick and three character deaths (all shown continuing on as ghosts), plus some mild swearing, drinking and smoking by adult and ghost characters, and a brief moment of animated cleavage. Underneath the effects, it's a sweet story about friendship, loss, and a father-daughter bond, with balanced roles for its female characters.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/casper","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":930,"t":"Escape to Witch Mountain (1995)","y":"1995","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. This ABC television remake of the Disney classic follows two orphaned siblings with psychic powers who go on the run after a greedy millionaire tries to exploit their abilities. Described as mild family adventure fare with a hostage situation and a confrontation with the antagonist rather than graphic peril. It carries the same underlying themes as the original: family loyalty, kindness, and using one's gifts responsibly.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escape_to_Witch_Mountain_(1995_film)","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Television"},{"num":931,"t":"FernGully 2: The Magical Rescue","y":"1998","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"In this direct-to-video sequel, the fairies of FernGully venture into the human world to rescue baby animals captured by poachers who've also set the rainforest on fire. There's some real peril here, including caged and nearly-drowning animals and a villain who whips his dogs, though nothing especially graphic and no bad language. The film leans hard into pro-conservation, anti-animal-testing messaging, continuing the original's environmental themes for a younger audience.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ferngully-2-the-magical-rescue","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":932,"t":"Warriors of Virtue","y":"1997","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. A middle-schooler with a leg injury is pulled into a Taoist-inspired fantasy world where anthropomorphic kangaroo warriors battle a tyrannical sorcerer threatening to invade Earth next. Expect stylized martial-arts fighting and fantasy peril throughout, in line with its PG rating, though nothing graphic. The story leans into themes of courage and pushing past physical limits as the boy finds his footing among the warriors.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warriors_of_Virtue","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Action"],"studio":"Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer"},{"num":933,"t":"Turbo: A Power Rangers Movie","y":"1997","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"Frequent stylized fights fill this Power Rangers spinoff, with the team punching, kicking, and using crossbows against villains, plus a tense scene involving a giant snake and characters dangling near lava. Language stays mild and the villain's costume is a bit more revealing than typical kids' fare. On the plus side, the Rangers consistently look out for each other and model teamwork under pressure, even if critics found the story thin.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/turbo-a-power-rangers-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Saban Entertainment"},{"num":934,"t":"Pippi Longstocking","y":"1997","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"This musical take on Astrid Lindgren's story opens with Pippi's sea-captain father swept overboard in a storm, and comic thieves take some pratfall tumbles, but violence stays very mild. It touches on the loss of both parents in a way sensitive kids might need help processing, though the tone stays playful overall. The film celebrates Pippi's independence and generosity while also showing that reuniting with family feels good in the end.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/pippi-longstocking","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Nelvana"},{"num":935,"t":"Mighty Morphin Power Rangers: The Movie","y":"1995","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"Like the TV series, this spinoff is full of cartoonish martial-arts combat as the Rangers punch, kick, and spin their way through fights with a rampaging monster and the villain Ivan Ooze. Insults stay silly rather than harsh, though one warrior character's outfit is fairly revealing. The film underscores teamwork and persistence, showing the heroes keep working together even after losing their usual powers.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/mighty-morphin-power-rangers-the-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Saban Entertainment"},{"num":936,"t":"Balto","y":"1995","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Based on the real 1925 diphtheria serum run, this animated adventure follows a half-wolf sled dog who is shunned for being different but proves himself by saving sick children. There's real tension in the ice and blizzard sequences, including near-falls off cliffs and a tense grizzly bear encounter, plus a somber moment showing coffins being readied for children who might not survive. The film is fundamentally about accepting outsiders and how friends supporting each other can be as important as any single act of heroism.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/balto","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":937,"t":"Dennis the Menace","y":"1993","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This live-action take on the comic strip leans hard into slapstick, with Mr. Wilson repeatedly on the receiving end of Dennis's mishaps, including one gag that leaves his face bloodied and swollen. There's also a kidnapping subplot involving a menacing drifter, some crude jokes about bodies and grown-ups, and background references to beer and cigarettes. Positive messaging is thin, though the film does end with Dennis and his grumpy neighbor finding a way to get along.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dennis-the-menace","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":938,"t":"Flipper","y":"1996","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"A New York City kid staying with his uncle in Florida befriends a dolphin, though the story opens with another dolphin being shot, and a hammerhead shark attack ramps up tension later on. The uncle is frequently shown drinking beer and smoking cigars, and a bit of mild profanity turns up in the dialogue. The film's heart is really about protecting fragile ocean ecosystems, with the boy and his uncle modeling environmental responsibility together.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/flipper","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":939,"t":"Jungle 2 Jungle","y":"1997","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"A businessman brings his jungle-raised son to New York in this broad comedy, where Russian mob characters wave around guns and knives, though it's all played for laughs rather than real menace. The bigger issue is how heavily the humor leans on ethnic stereotyping. Underneath the slapstick, there's a father-son reconciliation arc, even if the film doesn't dwell on it much.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jungle-2-jungle","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":940,"t":"Tom and Huck","y":"1995","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"This Disney adaptation of Twain's novel keeps the book's darker edge, including a stabbing death the boys witness, a villain who threatens children with a knife and gun, and references to child abuse. Expect some mild cursing, adult drinking, and a boy shown smoking a pipe, along with an ethnic stereotype in its central villain that hasn't aged well. That said, the film does champion loyalty, courage, and doing right even when it's frightening, as Tom learns what real friendship means.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/tom-and-huck","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":941,"t":"We're Back! A Dinosaur's Story","y":"1993","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Four time-traveling dinosaurs land in 1990s New York in this Amblin production, and while it's mostly playful, a scheming circus ringmaster creates genuinely frightening scenes meant to prey on kids' fears, including fire-breathing effects and mind-altering treats. Language stays very mild. Underneath the scares, the film reassures kids that fear doesn't have to control them, and its dinosaur heroes model bravery, kindness, and resourcefulness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/were-back-a-dinosaurs-story","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":942,"t":"Wild America","y":"1997","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. Based on the real Stouffer brothers, this film follows three siblings road-tripping across the American West in 1967 to film wildlife, running into alligators, wolves, bears, and rattlesnakes along the way. The encounters generate real suspense and a few close calls, though the violence stays at an adventure-peril level rather than anything graphic. It's ultimately a story about brotherhood, ambition, and respect for the natural world.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_America_(film)","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":943,"t":"Cats Don't Dance","y":"1997","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"This animated musical follows a hopeful cat trying to break into 1930s Hollywood, who's sabotaged by a jealous child star with a nasty private side. There's little violence or language to worry about, just a mildly menacing villain and some slapstick chaos. Its heart is squarely about pursuing dreams despite setbacks and judging people by character rather than image.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/cats-dont-dance","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":944,"t":"The Land Before Time II: The Great Valley Adventure","y":"1994","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"The dinosaur friends from the original film return to protect a hatchling that turns out to be a baby Sharptooth, with the bigger dinosaurs growling and baring teeth and a rockslide creating some peril. Content stays mild otherwise, appropriate for young viewers. The film keeps its predecessor's message about teamwork and accepting those who are different, with Littlefoot again modeling responsibility and compassion.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-land-before-time-ii-the-great-valley-adventure","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":945,"t":"The Land Before Time III: The Time of the Great Giving","y":"1995","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"A meteorite strike dries up the Great Valley's water, sparking territorial squabbles and recurring bullying among three young dinosaurs while the whole herd faces a drought. Only mild peril (a mudslide, a threatening trio of tyrannosaurs) and occasional name-calling, with no language or substance-use concerns. The film models cooperation, admitting when you're wrong, and working through scarcity together, and the kids display a strong sense of fairness throughout.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-land-before-time-iii-the-time-of-great-giving","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":946,"t":"The Land Before Time IV: Journey Through the Mists","y":"1996","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. This direct-to-video sequel is aimed at a preschool-through-early-grade audience, with the young dinosaurs braving a foggy, dangerous marshland to find medicine for an ailing grandparent. The main thread is Littlefoot's friend Ali learning to accept a stranger who looks and acts differently rather than fear her, in keeping with the franchise's mild-peril, message-driven formula. No language or substance-use content is noted.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Land_Before_Time_IV:_Journey_Through_the_Mists","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":947,"t":"Quest for Camelot","y":"1998","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"A spirited teenage girl sets out to recover her late father's stolen sword and save Camelot, aided by a blind former squire and a bumbling two-headed dragon. A couple of tense scenes and the death of a sympathetic character could rattle sensitive kids, though there's no bad language or substance use. Positive themes include teamwork, loyalty, and recognizing the strengths of people with disabilities, embodied by the resourceful blind hero.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/quest-for-camelot","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":948,"t":"Pokémon: The First Movie","y":"1998","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Scientists clone a hyper-powerful Pokémon named Mewtwo, whose fury at his creators erupts into near-constant battles, explosions, and a stretch where the hero appears to die. The fast, dark, storm-heavy action could overwhelm kids too young to separate real from pretend danger, though there's no bad language or substance use. The film argues that fighting solves nothing and that character matters more than origin, with the young hero modeling courage and compassion.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/pokemon-the-first-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Action","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":949,"t":"The Land Before Time V: The Mysterious Island","y":"1997","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. A swarm of pests strips the Great Valley of food, forcing the herd to migrate and sparking arguments over what's left; the kids sneak off at night and end up stranded on an island after a wave destroys their path home. Expect the series' typical chase-and-peril sequences rather than graphic violence. The story centers on resourcefulness and sticking together when resources run short.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Land_Before_Time_V:_The_Mysterious_Island","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":950,"t":"The Land Before Time VI: The Secret of Saurus Rock","y":"1998","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. Young dinosaurs fall from a rock formation and are chased by an Allosaurus and a Tyrannosaurus, plus a tornado strikes the valley -- peril typical of the franchise, including an implied predator death that could unsettle very young viewers. One of the weaker sequels, but it keeps the series' usual message about looking past hero-worship and relying on your community.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Land_Before_Time_VI:_The_Secret_of_Saurus_Rock","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":951,"t":"An American Tail: Fievel Goes West","y":"1991","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"The Mousekewitz mouse family heads west chasing a promised cat-free frontier, only to find new predators and swindlers waiting. A fair amount of cartoon-style peril between cats, dogs, and mice, none of it graphic, though the film's dated, stereotyped portrayals of Eastern European Jewish immigrants and Native American characters are the bigger concern. Musical numbers and a friendship-and-looking-out-for-each-other message balance an occasionally grating comic-relief subplot.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/an-american-tail-fievel-goes-west","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Western"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":952,"t":"Baby's Day Out","y":"1994","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"A wealthy infant is kidnapped by three bumbling crooks, escapes, and toddles unsupervised through city traffic and a construction site while his pursuers suffer one slapstick injury after another. The head-bonk, groin-hit style physical comedy and the baby's string of dangerous near-misses without a scratch could send a confusing safety message. The film's point about valuing family over money lands weakly, and there's no strong adult role model in the story.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/babys-day-out","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":953,"t":"Blank Check","y":"1994","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"An overlooked 11-year-old cashes a criminal's blank check for a million dollars and buys himself a mansion and toys, drawing the attention of both the mobster and an FBI agent posing as a love interest. Gun threats, a couple of scuffles, adult drinking at a party, and a jarring moment where a grown woman kisses the boy on the mouth, along with some innuendo. The story does land on family mattering more than money and windfalls revealing real friends, though the dad's early neglect and the boy's scheming are shakier role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/blank-check","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":954,"t":"The Borrowers","y":"1997","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A four-inch-tall family living secretly inside a normal house must stop a scheming lawyer from evicting the humans upstairs, leading to inventive traps, chemical mishaps, and slapstick chases. The peril has real (if non-fatal) stakes -- burns, prong pokes -- plus very mild language and a cigar-smoking villain who ends up doused in cheese. The film is praised for its decisive, capable female Borrowers and its messages about teamwork and family loyalty.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-borrowers","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"PolyGram Films"},{"num":955,"t":"Doug's 1st Movie","y":"1999","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Doug and Skeeter try to protect a gentle lake monster from a scheming, publicity-hungry town boss, all while Doug tries to work up the nerve to ask his crush to the school dance. Mild action (sharpshooters targeting a mechanical monster at the dance) and some spooky noises at the lake, with no language or substance-use content. The film's message about doing the right thing and protecting the environment comes through clearly, even if the story feels more like a stretched TV episode.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dougs-1st-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":956,"t":"Dunston Checks In","y":"1996","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"An orangutan smuggled into a posh hotel by a jewel thief instead befriends a neglected boy, leading to chandelier swinging, cigar smoking, and a tranquilizer-gun chase. Mild profanity, some drinking, and rough slapstick tussling (ear-biting, mock karate), plus mild peril that could worry kids sensitive about the animal's safety. Underneath the antics is a workaholic single dad learning to put his sons first.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dunston-checks-in","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":957,"t":"First Kid","y":"1996","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"A Secret Service agent is assigned to guard the rebellious teenage son of the president and ends up teaching him to stand up to bullies, ask out a girl, and just act like a normal kid. Comedic pratfalls, guns drawn during a climactic scene, a brief mooning gag, mild language, and Secret Service agents shown drinking beer, alongside an online-stalker subplot. At its core the film is about compassionate mentorship, with the boy growing from bratty to likeable as he's given room to be a regular teenager.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/first-kid","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":958,"t":"The Flintstones","y":"1994","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This live-action take on the cartoon town of Bedrock includes some cartoonish but occasionally rough violence like clubbing and a hanging threat, plus beer, martinis, and cigarettes shown around town. A flirty secretary delivers a few sexually loaded lines to Fred, and the whole film leans hard on old-fashioned gender roles for its women. There's a mild theme about sacrificing for friends, but the movie doesn't offer much in the way of real role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-flintstones","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":959,"t":"Jingle All the Way","y":"1996","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Arnold Schwarzenegger's frantic hunt for a sold-out toy leads to plenty of slapstick brawling, a tasering, and even a bomb scare with police drawing weapons, all played for laughs. Mild language, an outdated ethnic joke, some drinking and cigar smoking, and a subplot flirting with infidelity. The movie gestures toward a message about family mattering more than stuff, though the frantic toy chase mostly undercuts it, and the dad is a flawed model who lies his way through the day.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jingle-all-the-way","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Holiday"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":960,"t":"Sister Act","y":"1992","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"The story opens with an off-screen mob killing and includes gunplay and a kidnapping threat, plus some strong language and casual references to drinking and smoking around the casino scenes. Light kissing and a few mild sexual references. On the positive side, the convent's nuns are drawn as individuals rather than a stereotype, and the film centers on teamwork, community pride, and using your talents to lift others up.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/sister-act","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Buena Vista Pictures"},{"num":961,"t":"Sister Act 2: Back in the Habit","y":"1993","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Much gentler than the original, this sequel drops the crime and violence entirely, leaving only occasional mild language and some reckless driving played for comedy. Deloris's nun impersonation is treated as good-hearted rather than deceptive. The movie leans fully into positive territory, celebrating school participation, follow-through, and a diverse, well-run inner-city high school where one person's effort makes a real difference.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/sister-act-2-back-in-the-habit","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Buena Vista Pictures"},{"num":962,"t":"Miracle on 34th Street","y":"1994","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This remake has a store Santa knocking down a rival with his cane, some mild insults, and background drinking among department-store Santas and one drunk impostor. A grown-up romance with kissing, a hinted pregnancy, and a brief moment of nudity, alongside a cynical corporate-greed subplot. Its heart is still in the right place, championing kindness, imagination, and faith in things you can't prove.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/miracle-on-34th-street-1994","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Holiday","Comedy"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":963,"t":"North","y":"1994","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Not on CSM; substitute source used. In this widely panned satire, a neglected boy declares himself a free agent and auditions new parents around the world, encountering broad cultural caricatures along the way, including mockery of Amish life that drew criticism. Content-wise it's mild -- a handful of mild curses, no violence or sexual content -- but its ethnic stereotyping is its biggest problem. Underneath the satire, the story does land on a sincere message about appreciating the parents you already have.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_(1994_film)","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":964,"t":"Night at the Museum","y":"2006","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"Once the museum exhibits wake up, there's a lot of cartoonish fighting, chasing, and prop-weapon mayhem, plus a few scary beats like a looming dinosaur skeleton and an Attila the Hun chase that could rattle younger kids. Language stays mild, an adult has a glass of wine, and a couple of cultures get flattened into stereotypes. The story rewards Larry for pursuing his dreams, taking responsibility for his family, and learning through history, with Teddy Roosevelt as an encouraging mentor.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/night-at-the-museum","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":965,"t":"Night at the Museum: Battle of the Smithsonian","y":"2009","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"This sequel keeps up the chasing and prop-weapon threats, adds a genuinely creepy giant octopus and an eerie underworld sequence, and includes mild language plus some passionate kissing and light innuendo between adult characters. It stays true to its message about being yourself and doing what you love, with Amelia Earhart standing out as a spirited, independent role model who helps the hero grow from self-centered to community-minded.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/night-at-the-museum-battle-of-the-smithsonian","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":966,"t":"The Polar Express","y":"2004","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"The train ride is full of genuine peril -- runaway cars, falls, cracking ice, and treacherous climbs -- and a dark detour with a puppet-controlling stranger surrounded by unsettling, human-like broken toys can spook sensitive kids. No language, substance use, or sexual content anywhere in the film. What it does deliver in spades is a message about believing in things you can't see, the value of friendship and courage, and the real meaning of the holiday living in your heart.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-polar-express","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Holiday"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":967,"t":"Curious George","y":"2006","la":"4+","ca":"4+","full":"This gentle, preschool-friendly adaptation has only slapstick monkey mischief for its rough edges, along with some light adult flirting that ends in a kiss and a moment where animal control takes George away that can feel emotionally intense for little ones. A bit of product placement and a small deception around a museum exhibit. Mostly, the film is a warm celebration of curiosity, loyalty, and the growing friendship between George and the Man in the Yellow Hat.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/curious-george-2006","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":968,"t":"Happy Feet","y":"2006","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Mumble's world includes real danger from leopard seals, orcas, and a fishing trawler, plus an unsettling hallucination sequence once he's trapped in captivity. Courtship among the penguins comes with a lot of suggestive talk and movement, some mild insults and crude language, and a few flattened cultural stereotypes among the supporting characters. Still, the film champions individuality, honesty, friendship, and environmental awareness through Mumble's determination to be himself.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/happy-feet","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":969,"t":"Happy Feet Two","y":"2011","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"This sequel is milder than the original, with peril coming from an icy trap, threatening predators, and background scenes of smaller sea creatures being eaten, plus a bit of mild name-calling and light romantic innuendo between penguin couples. It leans heavily into positive territory, with strong themes about environmental awareness, chasing your own passions, and family and friends supporting each other through hard times.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/happy-feet-two","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":970,"t":"Star Wars: The Clone Wars","y":"2008","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This animated Star Wars entry is packed with near-nonstop laser and lightsaber battles between clones, droids, and Separatist forces, with characters destroyed on screen though without blood. Young Anakin is shown eating live bugs, a gag that could tempt imitation. Good and evil are drawn in fairly stark terms except for Anakin's simmering inner conflict, and the Republic is shown as a proudly multicultural coalition of species working together.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/star-wars-clone-wars","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Action","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Lucasfilm"},{"num":971,"t":"Corpse Bride","y":"2005","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"Tim Burton's stop-motion fairy tale leans into playful-creepy imagery: talking skeletons, decaying corpses, and an eyeball that pops out to reveal a wisecracking maggot living inside the bride. It's more whimsical than frightening, with minimal language and only fantasy-context drinking among the dead. At its heart it's a gentle romance about love, selflessness, and choosing what truly matters over status.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/tim-burtons-corpse-bride","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":972,"t":"ParaNorman","y":"2012","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"This stop-motion horror-comedy gets genuinely eerie, with zombies, ghosts, and unsettling imagery plus a dark backstory involving the execution of a child centuries earlier. Schoolyard name-calling, an implied curse word, and teens who drink and flirt. The payoff is a heartfelt message about empathy, courage, and giving misunderstood kids the benefit of the doubt, delivered partly through a supporting gay character treated with easy, matter-of-fact humor.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/paranorman","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Horror"],"studio":"Focus Features"},{"num":973,"t":"Isle of Dogs","y":"2018","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Wes Anderson's stop-motion tale is edgier than typical family animation, with dogfights, a disturbing image of a boy with debris lodged in his head, an unsettling sushi-prep scene, occasional coarse language, and adult drinking and pipe smoking. A suicide is referenced. Underneath the darker edges are messages about uncovering lies, redemption through sacrifice, loyalty, and one character using her voice against corruption and abuse of power.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/isle-of-dogs","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Fox Searchlight Pictures"},{"num":974,"t":"Diary of a Wimpy Kid","y":"2021","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This animated take on the book series keeps things light, with bike wipeouts, a broken arm, food fights, shoving, and a chaotic Halloween chase, plus mild insults like 'moron' and some bathroom humor. The boys occasionally fib to their parents, but the film centers on the idea that true friendship matters more than popularity, with Greg and Rowley having each other's backs through middle-school chaos.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/diary-of-a-wimpy-kid-0","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":976,"t":"The Bad Guys 2","y":"2025","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"The reformed crew is back for a heist-caper sequel loaded with chases, explosions, and slapstick, including characters getting drugged and tranquilized, plus mild swearing and a hinted romance between two animal characters. As with the first film, it champions teamwork, self-control, and the idea that people (and wolves) can genuinely change, with strong female leaders again at the center of the story.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-bad-guys-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Action"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":977,"t":"Minions: The Rise of Gru","y":"2022","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This Despicable Me prequel is wall-to-wall slapstick and martial-arts action, including a tied-up torture threat and a near-miss with crocodiles, all played for laughs and survived unharmed. A few brief rear-end gags and mild insults. Even with its villain-glorifying premise, it lands on messages about loyalty, teamwork, and mentorship, though its depiction of Chinese culture leans stereotypical and exoticized.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/minions-the-rise-of-gru","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Action"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":978,"t":"Sonic the Hedgehog","y":"2020","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"The live-action debut of Sega's speedy hero features cartoonish weapon battles that leave no one really hurt, one crude gesture, a single use of 'hell,' and a female character shown in revealing clothing plus a played-for-laughs marriage-and-kids fantasy sequence. The story is fairly thin on real takeaways, with characters too one-dimensional to serve as strong role models and some dated gender stereotyping.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/sonic-the-hedgehog-the-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":979,"t":"Sonic the Hedgehog 2","y":"2022","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"The sequel ramps up fantasy peril with near-drownings and characters getting struck by vehicles, though nothing feels graphically real, plus some mild swearing, fart jokes, and adults drinking on a stressful Hawaii trip. The film's throughline is that heroism finds you rather than the other way around, with strong messages about teamwork, family bonds, and Sonic winning over a former enemy through compassion.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/sonic-the-hedgehog-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":980,"t":"Sonic the Hedgehog 3","y":"2024","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"This installment hits noticeably harder than its predecessors, with a drone attack, a hospitalization, and two character deaths including a child, alongside mild swearing and emotionally heavy material around a captured character's mistreatment that may frighten younger viewers. The film's core message is that people are defined by the choices they make, reinforced through family, teamwork, and open communication among the leads.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/sonic-the-hedgehog-3","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Adventure"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":981,"t":"The Super Mario Bros. Movie","y":"2023","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"The video-game adaptation delivers fantasy action violence like missile fire and a finger-crushing piano gag, plus a genuinely startling zombie-skeleton sequence and a bit of schoolyard-style insult humor. Power-up mushrooms stand in for the game's classic mechanic. The story rewards perseverance, teamwork, and courage, with Peach portrayed as brave and proactive rather than a passive damsel alongside Mario.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-super-mario-bros-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":982,"t":"The Super Mario Galaxy Movie","y":"2026","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"This sequel leans into frequent fantasy-action peril, with menacing creatures, a kidnapping plot, magical weapon attacks, and a skeleton-emerging-from-lava moment that could unsettle younger kids, plus mild insults and a casino scene with implied alcoholic drinks. Its heart is a story about teamwork and the importance of family and friends, and it gives Bowser a redemption arc about learning to change for the better.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-super-mario-galaxy-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":983,"t":"Transformers One","y":"2024","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"An animated origin story set on Cybertron following miner-bots Orion Pax and D-16 before they become Optimus Prime and Megatron. Frequent, explosive sci-fi battles with characters crushed or dismembered, plus some mild swearing and heavy Hasbro toy marketing throughout. On the plus side, the film champions teamwork, courage, and the idea that physical strength isn't what makes a true leader.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/transformers-one","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Sci-Fi","Action"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":984,"t":"IF","y":"2024","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"A blend of live action and animated imaginary friends, following a girl coping with her mother's death and her father's heart surgery, both shown in flashback. A few jump scares and one use of a stronger swear word, but nothing graphic. The story leans into grief, curiosity, and using imagination as a healthy way to process hard emotions.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/if","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Comedy"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":985,"t":"KPop Demon Hunters","y":"2025","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"An animated adventure about a K-pop girl group who moonlight as demon fighters, battling soul-stealing creatures and a menacing demon king. Fights are stylized rather than graphic, though shadowy villains and tense confrontations can feel intense for younger kids, and there's some flirtatious byplay between two characters. The trio models perseverance, self-acceptance, and leaning on friends to face what scares you.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/kpop-demon-hunters","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Sony Pictures Animation"},{"num":986,"t":"Diary of a Wimpy Kid: The Last Straw","y":"2025","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Greg Heffley's dad threatens military school unless Greg can prove he's capable of real responsibility, leading to a wilderness-camp showdown. Cartoonish mishaps like a dog bite and a wasp's-nest tumble, plus mild insults and some brief comic nudity, all played for laughs. The film's heart is showing that shortcuts and lying backfire, while patient parenting and effort pay off.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/diary-of-a-wimpy-kid-the-last-straw","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"20th Century Animation"},{"num":987,"t":"Smurfs","y":"2025","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"This reboot mixes animation, live action, and musical numbers as the Smurfs face off against dark wizards who threaten to destroy them. The word 'Smurf' stands in for harsher language throughout, and there's some peril including a fall and menacing magical portals. Messages about self-belief, teamwork, and finding your own strengths run through the story.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/smurfs","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Adventure"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":988,"t":"The SpongeBob Movie: Search for SquarePants","y":"2025","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"SpongeBob sets out with Patrick to prove his courage after the ghostly Flying Dutchman tricks him into a series of deep-sea trials. Slapstick mishaps, toilet humor, and some genuinely spooky creatures (a three-headed bird, skeleton guards) round out the adventure. Underneath the gags, it reassures kids that being scared doesn't make you any less brave.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-spongebob-movie-search-for-squarepants","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":989,"t":"Gabby's Dollhouse: The Movie","y":"2025","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"Gabby and her grandmother Gigi chase down Gabby's runaway dollhouse after it falls into the hands of an eccentric cat-loving stranger. Several intense chase and jump-scare moments, plus a brief gag where a character acts woozy after sniffing catnip. The film centers a loving, multigenerational family and shows its young heroine leading with bravery and compassion.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/gabbys-dollhouse-the-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":990,"t":"Swapped","y":"2026","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A curious woodland creature and a small bird accidentally trade bodies and have to team up to survive predators and a wildfire while figuring out how to switch back. The peril feels real but isn't graphic, and mild insults are the extent of the rough language. It's ultimately about empathy, cooperation with nature, and understanding someone by walking in their world.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/swapped","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Netflix"},{"num":991,"t":"GOAT","y":"2026","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"An underdog goat chases his dream of going pro in a rough, full-contact animal sport called roarball. Competitive shoving and taunting on the field plus a few mentions of a parent's death, but nothing graphic, and the humor stays mild. The story pushes back against being judged by your size and celebrates a devoted single dad and hard-won self-belief.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/goat-0","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Sports","Comedy"],"studio":"Sony Pictures Animation"},{"num":992,"t":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles II: The Secret of the Ooze","y":"1991","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"The Turtles face off against two new mutant villains created by Shredder using the same ooze that made them who they are. Fight scenes are choreographed and bloodless, though the monster designs might startle very young viewers, and there's a brief scene of a minor character smoking. Splinter's mentorship and the Turtles' loyalty to each other anchor the film's message about teamwork.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/teenage-mutant-ninja-turtles-2-the-secret-of-the-ooze","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Comedy"],"studio":"New Line Cinema"},{"num":993,"t":"Beethoven's 2nd","y":"1993","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"The lovable St. Bernard is back with a mate and puppies in this family comedy, but one subplot -- a teen party scene where a drunken boy nearly assaults a girl before the dog interrupts -- is a jarring, out-of-place inclusion for what's otherwise a kids' movie. Teen drinking at that party and a mild, awkwardly handled conversation about where puppies come from. On a lighter note, the film follows the family learning to take responsibility for the new litter.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/beethovens-2nd","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":994,"t":"Mr. Popper's Penguins","y":"2011","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"A career-focused real estate developer's life is upended when he inherits a family of penguins, forcing him to slow down and reconnect with his kids. Mostly harmless slapstick, though there's a joke about erectile-dysfunction medication and champagne at a party that adults will catch and kids will miss. The throughline is a familiar but effective lesson about choosing family over ambition.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/mr-poppers-penguins","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":995,"t":"We Bought a Zoo","y":"2011","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"This warm family dramedy follows a widowed dad and his kids restoring a rundown zoo, but the emotional core involves grief over the mother's death that can hit younger viewers hard, plus more profanity than typical for a PG movie, a disturbingly violent teen drawing, and some adult social drinking. On the plus side it models open family communication, teamwork, and resilience through loss.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/we-bought-a-zoo","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Drama"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":996,"t":"Earth to Echo","y":"2014","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"Four kids team up to help a stranded alien get home in this E.T.-style adventure shot in a shaky found-footage style, with mild peril, a bar and party scene where alcohol appears in the background, some near-swearing and name-calling, and innocent flirting. The story rewards loyalty, courage, and friends looking out for each other.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/earth-to-echo","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Relativity Media"},{"num":997,"t":"The House with a Clock in Its Walls","y":"2018","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Directed by horror filmmaker Eli Roth as a gentler 'starter' spookfest, it's full of creepy imagery and jump scares, living dolls, a corpse, a graveyard scene, a bullying incident, and war-flashback references with blood, plus mild swearing. Underneath the frights, it champions individuality, perseverance, and found family.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-house-with-a-clock-in-its-walls","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":998,"t":"Diary of a Wimpy Kid: Rodrick Rules","y":"2011","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"Greg navigates seventh-grade sibling rivalry with older brother Rodrick, featuring schoolyard-style insults, mild physical scuffling, and a teen party scene where cup contents are never specified as alcoholic. It leans into gentle comedy about family embarrassment rather than anything harsh, and Greg ultimately learns to value being himself over chasing popularity.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/diary-of-a-wimpy-kid-rodrick-rules","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":999,"t":"Mirror Mirror","y":"2012","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This lighter, comedic spin on Snow White includes bloodless sword fights, menacing puppet-like creatures, adult drinking at parties, and a few innuendos likely to fly over kids' heads. It reframes the princess as an active, capable heroine trained by the dwarfs in self-defense, warning against vanity and greed while celebrating kindness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/mirror-mirror","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Relativity Media"},{"num":1000,"t":"Barbie","y":"2023","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Greta Gerwig's satirical comedy tackles gender expectations and patriarchy with one bleeped strong swear word, milder profanity, cartoonish slapstick fights, party scenes with drinking, and some suggestive jokes and innuendo that skew older, plus heavy toy-brand promotion throughout. Its core message champions female empowerment, self-acceptance, and mutual support between women.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/barbie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1001,"t":"Roald Dahl's Matilda the Musical","y":"2022","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This musical adaptation includes a genuinely frightening tyrant headmistress who swings a child by the pigtails, locks kids in a spiked closet, and force-feeds one an entire cake, plus a scene where a circus performer falls and later dies in a hospital, alongside brief adult cocktail-drinking. It strongly promotes courage in facing bullies, love of reading, and standing up for others.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/roald-dahls-matilda-the-musical","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":1002,"t":"Wonka","y":"2023","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This musical origin story for the famous chocolatier includes a gunpoint threat with one shot fired, a near-drowning, an explosion, and a child locked in a birdcage-like coop, along with mild insults and one instance of a stronger word. A running joke about a character gaining extreme weight reads as fat-shaming, and one candy causes a simulated drunk effect, but themes of imagination, hope, perseverance, and generosity carry the story.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/wonka","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1003,"t":"Sing 2","y":"2021","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"The animated musical sequel has cartoonish, non-graphic peril like near-falls and a shock gag, mild insults, mentions of a character's grief over a late spouse, and a brief married-couple kiss, plus some intimidating villain characters that could unsettle sensitive kids. It emphasizes teamwork, honesty, and persistence in chasing your dreams.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/sing-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Comedy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":1004,"t":"The Garfield Movie","y":"2024","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"This animated adventure includes cartoon slapstick, a bird getting swallowed, an electrocution gag, and chases with intimidating adversaries, plus mild insults and one stronger British-style curse word. A scene of young Garfield left alone and frightened in a dark alley may affect sensitive kids, and heavy brand product placement runs throughout, but the story highlights teamwork, forgiveness, and family bonds.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-garfield-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":1005,"t":"The Angry Birds Movie","y":"2016","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"Based on the mobile game, it centers on an outcast bird who must overcome his anger to protect his island from invading pigs, with cartoon violence like explosions and characters launched into buildings, crude jokes, party drinking, and a voyeuristic gag. Some scenes imply real danger, but it ultimately encourages not judging others too quickly and taking personal responsibility.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-angry-birds-movie","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":1006,"t":"The Angry Birds Movie 2","y":"2019","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"The animated sequel pairs the birds and pigs against a new rival, with slapstick action, potty humor, and a handful of mildly suggestive jokes about mating season and nudity, though peril always resolves safely with no lasting harm. It builds on the original by emphasizing teamwork, diplomacy, accountability, and earning forgiveness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-angry-birds-movie-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Sony Pictures"},{"num":1007,"t":"Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle","y":"2017","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Teens get sucked into a video-game jungle where their avatars die and respawn repeatedly, including a jaguar mauling and a couple of jump scares, alongside scattered swearing and some body-swap humor about gender and anatomy. One sequence has teen avatars drinking cocktails and getting tipsy. On the plus side, the group has to lean on teamwork, honesty, and generosity to survive, and popularity turns out not to matter much once everyone is equally out of their depth.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jumanji-welcome-to-the-jungle","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":1008,"t":"Jumanji: The Next Level","y":"2019","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"The gang is pulled back into the game, this time with two grandfathers along for the ride, and again dies and regenerates through animal attacks, falls, and explosions without any blood shown. There's frequent strong language and a backstory involving a villain who killed a character's parents. The film keeps leaning on teamwork, courage, and empathy, showing that having people you can count on matters most when things get hard.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jumanji-the-next-level","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":1010,"t":"Ramona and Beezus","y":"2010","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This adaptation of Beverly Cleary's books follows spirited young Ramona as her family deals with her dad losing his job, all while she navigates squabbles with her older sister. Content is mild: a bit of slapstick, a kitchen mishap, a gently handled pet death, and a couple of light adult and teen kisses. The film centers family loyalty, staying true to yourself, and finding the bright side of hard times.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ramona-and-beezus","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":1011,"t":"Zathura: A Space Adventure","y":"2005","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"Two feuding brothers get pulled into a wild game that lands their house in the middle of space, complete with armed ships, hostile aliens, and a malfunctioning robot. Some mild profanity, plenty of peril and explosions, and a bit of physical fighting between the siblings. Like its cousin Jumanji, the danger forces the brothers to stop competing and start looking out for each other.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/zathura-a-space-adventure","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":1012,"t":"My Girl","y":"1991","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Eleven-year-old Vada, who is still processing her mother's death, spends a formative summer with her best friend Thomas J. before a sudden, off-screen death shakes her world again. Some mild swearing and a passing reference to marijuana, but the emotional weight of grief is the real content note here. The film handles loss with care, showing kids that life continues and that talking through pain with family helps.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/my-girl","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":1013,"t":"My Girl 2","y":"1994","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"Now a bit older, Vada travels to Los Angeles to learn more about the mother she never knew, while also dealing with the recent loss of her best friend and her dad's job as an undertaker. Mild language shows up, a hippie character is shown smoking pot, and two teens sneak out at night, but there's no violence. Every parental figure in the story is loving and engaged, and the film centers on self-discovery and taking pride in where you come from.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/my-girl-2","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":1014,"t":"The Secret Garden","y":"1993","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This is Agnieszka Holland's well-known adaptation, following orphaned Mary Lennox as she's sent to live with her withdrawn uncle and discovers a locked-away garden that helps heal both her and her sickly cousin. Content concerns are light: an earthquake causes the parents' deaths before the story begins, there's a slap and some unkind name-calling, and the pacing is slow and quiet rather than action-driven. The film's real strength is showing how nature, curiosity, and friendship can pull spoiled, grieving children out of their shells.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-secret-garden","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1015,"t":"Life-Size","y":"2000","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A grieving girl accidentally magics her fashion doll into a living person, played by Tyra Banks, leading to a fish-out-of-water comedy about what actually makes someone whole. Mild peril, some flirty adult humor and a near-kiss, and the doll-world segments lean into dated ideas about shopping and appearance. Still, the film pushes back on those stereotypes some by having its young lead prefer football to fashion, and it lands on messages about trying your best and valuing connection over perfection.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/life-size","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Television"},{"num":1016,"t":"Ella Enchanted","y":"2004","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"In this loose, comic take on the Cinderella story, Ella is cursed at birth to obey any command given to her and has to find a way to break the spell before it forces her to harm the prince she loves. The peril and villainy (an ogre, a menacing curse, a mother's illness) are played mostly for laughs rather than real scares, and a fairy character gets comically drunk at one point. The film's heart is about reclaiming your own agency and choosing kindness, with good characters rewarded and cruelty ultimately punished.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ella-enchanted","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Comedy"],"studio":"Miramax Films"},{"num":1017,"t":"How to Eat Fried Worms","y":"2006","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"A new kid at school gets dared into eating ten worms in a single day after a bully sets out to humiliate him, and the film leans hard into gross-out, potty-style humor along the way. Bullying and name-calling drive much of the plot, though it stays free of real violence. The takeaway is a fairly earnest one about facing down bullies with courage instead of running, backed by a supportive, understanding family.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/how-to-eat-fried-worms","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"New Line Cinema"},{"num":1018,"t":"Where the Wild Things Are","y":"2009","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"Spike Jonze's adaptation follows a lonely, misbehaving boy named Max who sails off to an island of temperamental monsters who crown him their king. The Wild Things smash and burn things when they lose their tempers, there's a tense storm sequence, some mild swearing, and an overall melancholy, muted tone that stays with real feelings of loneliness and insecurity rather than resolving neatly. It's ultimately about the messy, complicated nature of childhood emotions and the pull of home and family, carried by a Wild Thing who models inclusion even when others reject her.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/where-the-wild-things-are","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Drama"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1019,"t":"Adventures in Babysitting (1987)","y":"1987","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"This is the 1987 original, not the 2016 Disney Channel remake: a babysitter's night watching three kids spirals into an unplanned trip through downtown Chicago full of car chases, run-ins with a street gang, and mistaken danger from criminals who aren't actually after them. Frequent strong language, guns and knives brandished for tension, a scary high-rise ledge scene, and some innuendo including a college guy taking an interest in a 17-year-old. The night's chaos mostly plays out with the babysitter prioritizing the kids' safety, though the film doesn't dwell much on consequences for the risky choices made along the way.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/adventures-in-babysitting","addedAt":"2026-08-24","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Touchstone Pictures"},{"num":1020,"t":"To Kill a Mockingbird","y":"1962","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"A childhood memoir turned courtroom drama: young Scout Finch watches her lawyer father Atticus defend a Black man falsely accused of raping a white woman in Depression-era Alabama. This one runs genuinely heavy for a family list -- a racial slur is used repeatedly by the story's bigots, an angry mob confronts a jailed man, courtroom testimony touches on the assault (handled with period-era restraint), and a man is shot and killed near the end. Even so, it's held up as a landmark portrait of empathy and injustice: Atticus's insistence on understanding people before judging them, and Scout and Jem's own moral growth, make it best suited to older kids and teens ready for a serious conversation about racism.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/to-kill-a-mockingbird","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":1021,"t":"A Series of Unfortunate Events","y":"2004","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"Jim Carrey headlines this darkly comic adaptation of the Lemony Snicket books, following the three orphaned Baudelaire children as their conniving new guardian, Count Olaf, schemes to steal their fortune and get rid of them along the way. Expect near-constant peril and gothic, macabre humor rather than graphic violence -- parents die off-screen, kids are threatened and manipulated, and the mood stays gloomy throughout, which can unsettle more sensitive viewers even as older kids find it deliciously creepy. On the plus side, the Baudelaire siblings are resourceful, brave, and fiercely loyal to each other, and the underlying message is that persistence and cleverness can win out even when the adults around you won't listen.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/lemony-snickets-a-series-of-unfortunate-events","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":1022,"t":"Spy Kids 2: The Island of Lost Dreams","y":"2002","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"Carmen and Juni Cortez return as full-fledged junior spies, racing a rival sibling duo to a mysterious island crawling with hybrid creatures while trying to rescue their parents. There's plenty of gadget-fueled peril, chases, and gross-out creature gags, but nobody gets seriously hurt and the scary-looking stuff reliably turns out friendly; language stays essentially clean. It's praised for putting smart, capable Latino and female characters front and center, with teamwork and courage as the throughline -- a fun, low-worry sequel for the original's fans.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spy-kids-2-the-island-of-lost-dreams","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Miramax Films"},{"num":1023,"t":"Spy Kids 3-D: Game Over","y":"2003","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"Juni goes it mostly alone this time, diving into a deadly virtual-reality game to rescue his sister Carmen and stop a villainous Toymaker from taking over the world. The action is stylized and cartoonish -- giant robots, lava, exploding toy soldiers -- and stays within family-adventure bounds, with no real bloodshed or profanity to speak of, though it leans more on level-to-level spectacle than the earlier films' stronger plotting. Family loyalty and teamwork remain the throughline, and the wheelchair-using grandfather gets a meaningful, capable role, even if the messages are stated more than shown this time around.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spy-kids-3-d-game-over","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Miramax Films"},{"num":1024,"t":"Addams Family Values","y":"1993","la":"12+","ca":"13+","full":"This gleefully macabre sequel sends the Addams kids off to a chirpy summer camp while Uncle Fester falls for a gold-digging new bride with murder on her mind. The humor leans harder into innuendo and mock-violence than the original -- comedic near-electrocutions, a baby nearly tossed off a roof, and grown-up jokes that sail over young kids' heads but register with teens and parents. What keeps it from feeling mean-spirited is the Addams family's genuine, unconditional love for each other, framed as healthier than the smiling conformity of the outsiders mocking them -- a dark-humored but ultimately warm case for embracing being different.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/addams-family-values","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Paramount Pictures"},{"num":1025,"t":"High School Musical 3: Senior Year","y":"2008","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Troy, Gabriella, and the Wildcats face graduation, prom, and college decisions in this theatrical send-off to the Disney Channel trilogy, packed with big dance numbers. Content is very mild: a little flirting, a couple of kisses, and low-stakes teen drama over choosing between colleges and following your heart. Messages about supporting friends' dreams and staying true to yourself make it a safe, catchy pick for tweens.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/high-school-musical-3-senior-year","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Comedy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":1026,"t":"The Chronicles of Narnia: Prince Caspian","y":"2008","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"The Pevensie siblings are pulled back into Narnia to help young Prince Caspian reclaim his throne from a tyrannical uncle, and this sequel leans harder into war than the first film. Expect extended battle sequences and a brutal one-on-one duel, plus a light romantic thread that ends in a single kiss. The story rewards resisting revenge and pride, and owning up to mistakes, but the heavier combat makes it a better fit for kids past the youngest ages.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-chronicles-of-narnia-prince-caspian","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":1027,"t":"The Chronicles of Narnia: The Voyage of the Dawn Treader","y":"2010","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"Edmund, Lucy, and their whiny cousin Eustace join King Caspian's voyage to find seven lost lords, running into a giant sea serpent, an island where visitors go missing, and a genuinely unsettling green mist. There's sword fighting, hand-to-hand combat, and one on-screen death, alongside real scares for younger viewers in a nightmare sequence. The quest doubles as a lesson in facing down jealousy, greed, and self-doubt, which makes it worth a post-movie chat with older kids.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-chronicles-of-narnia-the-voyage-of-the-dawn-treader","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":1028,"t":"Scooby-Doo","y":"2002","la":"9+","ca":"10+","full":"The gang reunites to solve spooky goings-on at a resort island in this live-action take on the cartoon, blending genuinely creepy visuals with gross-out slapstick. Parents should know about a drug-adjacent sight gag, a scene implying Velma got drunk, a prolonged belching-and-farting contest, and general college-age partying and flirting. Underneath the crude jokes, the mystery still comes down to the gang working as a team and trusting each other.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/scooby-doo","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Mystery"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1029,"t":"Scooby-Doo 2: Monsters Unleashed","y":"2004","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"The Mystery Inc. gang returns for a sillier, effects-heavy sequel built around a costume-exhibit heist and cartoonish monsters come to life. Expect frequent potty humor, mildly scary ghoulish creatures, a bar scene with beer, and a quick joke about Shaggy sniffing something that smells like marijuana. Underneath the slapstick, it still carries the original show's message about teamwork and looking past first impressions.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/scooby-doo-2-monsters-unleashed","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Mystery"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1030,"t":"How the Grinch Stole Christmas","y":"2000","la":"6+","ca":"8+","full":"Ron Howard's live-action spin on the Dr. Seuss classic gives Jim Carrey's Grinch a manic, physically intense performance, with cartoonish fires, explosions, and falls, plus a few genuinely creepy moments and a threatening line aimed at kids. Mild crude language slips into the broad slapstick humor throughout. Despite the chaos, the story still lands its original message about kindness and community mattering more than presents.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/how-the-grinch-stole-christmas","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Holiday"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":1031,"t":"Charlie and the Chocolate Factory","y":"2005","la":"8+","ca":"9+","full":"Tim Burton's darker, more visually stylized take on Roald Dahl's story follows Charlie through Willy Wonka's factory alongside four misbehaving kids who each get a strange, fitting comeuppance -- one inflates like a giant blueberry, another is sucked up a pipe, another is dragged off by squirrels. The imagery leans more unsettling than the 1971 original, including an eerie flashback involving burned dolls. Charlie's own honesty and devotion to his struggling family anchor the film's message about character mattering more than greed.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/charlie-and-the-chocolate-factory-2005","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1032,"t":"Cheaper by the Dozen","y":"2003","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"Steve Martin and Bonnie Hunt play parents juggling a new coaching job, a book deal, and twelve kids, with the resulting chaos played for slapstick -- broken furniture, mild name-calling, and a running joke about a vasectomy. Adults are shown drinking champagne at a party, and mild language like \"damn\" pops up here and there. The family ultimately learns to put each other first over career ambition, a message about prioritizing family that anchors the comedy.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/cheaper-by-the-dozen","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":1033,"t":"Troop Beverly Hills","y":"1989","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"This 1989 fish-out-of-water comedy is a silly parody of Beverly Hills excess, following socialite Phyllis Nefler as she reluctantly takes over her daughter's Wilderness Girls troop. Concerns include her near-constant smoking and daytime wine drinking, crude language, innuendo-laden jokes, and a steady parade of luxury-brand name-dropping that glamorizes materialism. On the positive side, Phyllis grows from a self-absorbed trophy wife into a genuinely attentive leader, and the girls are encouraged to embrace their own quirks rather than conform.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/troop-beverly-hills","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":1034,"t":"Alexander and the Terrible, Horrible, No Good, Very Bad Day","y":"2014","la":"7+","ca":"8+","full":"Loosely adapting Judith Viorst's classic book, this Disney comedy piles an entire family's disasters into one single day for 11-year-old Alexander. Concerns include his teen sister accidentally getting drunk on cough syrup, their dad's shirt catching fire, a car fender-bender during a driving test, and a cruel prank where a boy's photo is composited onto pinup images and circulated at school. Underneath the chaos, the story lands a sweet, book-based message about families sticking together and everyone having a bad day sometimes.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/alexander-and-the-terrible-horrible-no-good-very-bad-day","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy"],"studio":"Walt Disney Pictures"},{"num":1035,"t":"Air Bud Returns","y":"2026","la":"6+","ca":"Not on CSM; substitute source used","full":"This franchise relaunch follows grieving 12-year-old Jacob, who finds his late father's VHS tape of the original Air Bud and befriends a stray golden retriever, and the two chase a basketball dream together with a scrappy underdog team. Content concerns mirror the earlier films: a child processing the death of a parent is a persistent emotional undercurrent, alongside typical sports-movie competition and mild schoolyard conflict. Positive messages echo the series' hallmarks -- perseverance, teamwork, and healing through connection with an animal companion and a found community.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Bud_Returns","addedAt":"2026-08-27","addedVia":"discovery","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Sports","Drama"],"studio":"Cineverse"},{"num":1036,"t":"Scoob!","y":"2020","la":"7","ca":"7","full":"\"Goofy Scooby reboot with hijinks and scenes of peril.\" There's cartoon chase-and-monster action (a giant three-headed dog, laser-shooting robots), name-calling insults, some gross-out humor, and a couple of mild innuendo lines. Positive: the gang leans on friendship, courage, and teamwork, and Shaggy discovers real bravery along the way.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/scoob","addedAt":"2026-08-28","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros."},{"num":1037,"t":"The Greatest Showman","y":"2017","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A dazzling, loosely biographical musical about circus founder P.T. Barnum's rise, built around big anthems about chasing dreams and embracing people who don't fit in. Expect some kissing and light romance, a bar fight, mild language, and scenes of prejudice and public shaming directed at the show's performers, alongside genuinely positive messages about self-acceptance, found family, and standing up to bullies.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-greatest-showman","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Drama"],"studio":"20th Century Fox"},{"num":1038,"t":"Annie","y":"2014","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"A modern-day update of the classic hard-knock-life musical, following foster kid Annie as she's temporarily taken in by a self-serving mayoral candidate. A boozy, hostile foster mother verbally torments the kids in her care, there are a couple of tense scenes where Annie is in danger, mild language, and heavy brand product placement -- but the film's core is about family, honesty, and hope winning out over greedy, scheming adults.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/annie-2014","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Comedy"],"studio":"Columbia Pictures"},{"num":1039,"t":"Wicked","y":"2024","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"Part one of the big-screen Broadway adaptation, tracing how green-skinned Elphaba and blonde Glinda go from rival roommates to unlikely friends before Elphaba is branded the \"Wicked Witch.\" There are light jump-scares (flying monkeys, the Wizard's giant floating head), bullying and prejudice aimed at Elphaba for being different, mild name-calling, and brief suggestive material, balanced against strong messages about accepting difference, questioning propaganda, and loyalty between friends.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/wicked","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":1040,"t":"Wicked: For Good","y":"2025","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"The darker second half of the Wicked story, as Elphaba is hunted as a fugitive and Glinda rises to power under the Wizard's regime. Expect fantasy and more realistic violence -- flying-monkey attacks, mob aggression, fights, pistol threats, and a magically created storm that results in a character's death -- plus heavier emotional stakes and more romance than part one. The throughline stays positive: who we become is shaped by our own choices, not other people's judgment, and no one is entirely good or wicked.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/wicked-for-good","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Universal Pictures"},{"num":1041,"t":"Pokémon Detective Pikachu","y":"2019","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A live-action mystery-adventure where a wisecracking Pikachu (voiced by Ryan Reynolds) teams up with a teenager to solve his missing father's disappearance in a city where humans and Pokémon live side by side. There's a lab fire and a fatal car crash early on, Pokémon battles that cause injuries and property damage, a tense chase through a shifting dreamlike landscape, and a couple of mild innuendos, alongside solid messages about teamwork, courage, and friendship. Resolved from a generic \"Pokémon movies\" request -- Detective Pikachu is the best-known Pokémon film not yet in the catalog (the 20+ numbered anime films are out of scope for one batch; \"Pokémon: The First Movie\" is already at num 948).","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/pokemon-detective-pikachu","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Mystery"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Pictures"},{"num":1042,"t":"Minions","y":"2015","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"Illumination's origin story for Gru's yellow henchmen follows a trio of Minions on a globe-trotting 1960s quest to find a new evil boss, landing them with supervillain Scarlet Overkill. It's mostly slapstick and gag-based, but there are cartoonish battles, freeze-ray zaps, torture-device sight gags, a few glimpses of bare Minion behinds, and mild infrequent insults like \"idiot\" and \"loser.\" The story leans into the appeal of being bad, but it also rewards loyalty, teamwork, and sticking with friends through failure.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/minions","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Illumination Entertainment"},{"num":1043,"t":"Saving Bikini Bottom: The Sandy Cheeks Movie","y":"2024","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"In this SpongeBob spinoff, marine biologist Sandy Cheeks gets to be the hero: when a corporate villain scoops up all of Bikini Bottom to harvest for a pet-transformation scheme, Sandy has to out-science and out-fight her way to a rescue. Peril stays cartoonish -- characters are cloned, trapped in a containment globe, launched from cannons, and chased by rattlesnakes -- and language is limited to mild insults and a fake-swear (\"holy sugar cookies\"). Sandy herself is a brave, brainy, athletic role model who leads with curiosity and teamwork.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/saving-bikini-bottom-the-sandy-cheeks-movie","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Nickelodeon Movies"},{"num":1044,"t":"Plankton: The Movie","y":"2025","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"This darker, musical SpongeBob spinoff centers on Plankton admitting his endless Krabby Patty schemes have wrecked his marriage to his AI wife Karen, who responds by turning into a world-conquering robot he has to stop with SpongeBob's help. A giant robot lays siege to Bikini Bottom with explosions and cannon fire, and Karen's monstrous robotic form may unsettle younger viewers, though the destruction stays cartoonish. Insults like \"moron\" and \"idiot\" show up but nothing stronger, and the film pushes a real message about owning your mistakes and choosing empathy over greed.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/plankton-the-movie","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Nickelodeon Movies"},{"num":1045,"t":"Extinct","y":"2021","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"This Netflix original follows donut-shaped siblings Ed and Op, the last of a species called flummels, who get flung from 1835 into present-day Shanghai and learn their whole kind died out while they were gone. It's a comic sci-fi adventure with cartoon slapstick violence and mild scares, though the siblings bicker and say hurtful things to each other along the way, and there's a running joke about the blue-footed booby. The heart of the film is Ed and Op learning to set aside their rivalry and work together, with a light nod to real ideas about extinction and conservation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/extinct","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Netflix Animation"},{"num":1046,"t":"Pets United","y":"2019","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"A stray dog named Roger, wrongly hunted by robot police in a city called Robo City, teams up with a friendly robot pet and a ragtag group of animals after the city's mayor exiles every non-robot resident. This leans hard into action for a family cartoon -- chases, fights, a dog shot with a net gun, flying knives, car and train crashes, and an animal seemingly crushed by a scrap-metal machine -- alongside mild insults and one mild swear. The underlying message is misfits banding together against an unjust system, aiming at loyalty and standing up for the vulnerable.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/pets-united","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Action"],"studio":"Serious Productions"},{"num":1047,"t":"Latte and the Magic Waterstone","y":"2019","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"A gentle computer-animated adventure about a brave young hedgehog named Latte who sets out to recover a stolen magic waterstone so her drought-stricken forest can have water again. There are a handful of playground-style scuffles (shoving, slapping), some verbal bullying about being different, and a few chase sequences where Latte and her friends are pursued by predators or tumble down cliffs -- all mild and cartoonish. Latte models courage, determination, and persistence, and the story's message is that natural resources like water belong to everyone, not the powerful few.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/latte-and-the-magic-waterstone","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"Dreamin' Dolphin Film"},{"num":1048,"t":"Arlo the Alligator Boy","y":"2021","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A sweet, goofy animated musical road-trip about a half-alligator, half-human boy who leaves the bayou to track down the father he's never known, picking up an oddball found family along the way. There's a shadowy, threatening \"beast\" that looms over much of the film, mild cartoon violence, a couple of adult-aimed jokes that will sail over kids' heads, and some old-fashioned Southern stereotypes among characters who want to exploit Arlo as a tourist attraction. Its heart is in the right place: messages of belonging, being your true self, and valuing friendship over wealth or fitting in.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/arlo-the-alligator-boy","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"Netflix Animation"},{"num":1049,"t":"Scrooge: A Christmas Carol","y":"2022","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"Netflix's big, energetic musical retelling of Dickens' classic -- Ebenezer Scrooge gets visited by three ghosts on Christmas Eve and is pushed toward redemption, set to original songs. The ghost sequences run genuinely eerie for younger viewers, Scrooge is comically electrocuted at one point, and there's slapstick violence and insult-style name-calling, plus a brief kiss and a passing mention of wine at dinner. The throughline is strongly positive: real change and generosity are possible for anyone willing to open their heart, even someone as miserly as Scrooge.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/scrooge-a-christmas-carol","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Musical","Holiday"],"studio":"Timeless Films"},{"num":1050,"t":"Hilda and the Mountain King","y":"2021","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"In this darker, feature-length continuation of the Netflix series, adventurous Hilda is magically swapped bodies with a troll baby -- she's stuck living as a troll in the eerie caves under the mountain while the troll now walks around in her old life with her mom. It leans into genuinely dark, atmospheric visuals and sound design from the opening scene on, with real dangers like falls from cliffs alongside fantastical threats of being stomped on or eaten by giant trolls, plus a sustained separation-anxiety thread. Hilda stays resourceful, empathetic, and brave throughout, with real emotional depth about identity, belonging, and what makes a family.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/hilda-and-the-mountain-king","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Silvergate Media"},{"num":1051,"t":"Dog Gone Trouble","y":"2021","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"A pampered dog named Trouble loses his cushy mansion life when his elderly owner dies and her greedy relatives dump him out of a moving van; lost in the woods, he has to team up with a ragtag pack of strays and a struggling street musician to find his way home. Expect dogs in peril throughout -- younger kids may find the premise sad -- plus animated skirmishes between the dogs and a gang of squirrels, close-call traffic scares, shouting matches, and scatological humor along with mild name-calling. The message lands well for elementary-age viewers: love, loyalty, and family matter more than money or material comfort.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dog-gone-trouble","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Vanguard Animation"},{"num":1052,"t":"Orion and the Dark","y":"2024","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"A Charlie Kaufman-written tale in which an anxiety-ridden 11-year-old boy is whisked away for a night with a talking personification of Dark itself, who wants to prove that nighttime isn't something to fear. It's funnier and more emotionally sophisticated than a typical kids' cartoon, but also more intense: Orion's fear-fueled imagination conjures menacing clowns, swarming bees, and a fall from a skyscraper, plus public humiliation and a school bully, with a handful of mild insults. The payoff is a genuinely useful message for anxious kids: facing fears head-on is more empowering than avoiding them.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/orion-and-the-dark","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Comedy"],"studio":"DreamWorks Animation"},{"num":1053,"t":"Octonauts & the Caves of Sac Actun","y":"2020","la":"4+","ca":"4+","full":"A feature-length outing for the preschool-friendly underwater rescue crew, in which Captain Barnacles and Peso escort a young octopus named Coba back to his Caribbean home, only to get diverted into a maze of flooded Mexican caves. The team faces cave-ins, dwindling air supply, and encounters with cave-dwelling predators -- played more seriously than a typical episode but never graphic. The Octonauts model calm teamwork, perseverance, and courage under pressure; note that the story stays tightly focused on their own crew rather than showing much curiosity about the people they encounter along the way.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/octonauts-the-caves-of-sac-actun","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Silvergate Media"},{"num":1054,"t":"Octonauts & the Ring of Fire","y":"2021","la":"4+","ca":"Not on CSM; substitute source used","full":"This Octonauts special sends the crew into the volcanically active Pacific \"Ring of Fire\" after a chain of undersea eruptions triggers tsunamis and strands ocean animals, including a beached whale the team races to save. It's TV-Y and stays true to the franchise's gentle preschool tone, but the disaster-movie premise -- erupting volcanoes, tidal waves, animals in real danger -- pushes the peril a notch higher than a typical episode. The throughline is classic Octonauts: quick thinking, ingenuity, and cooperative rescue work, with every creature saved by the end.","srcUrl":"https://octonauts.fandom.com/wiki/The_Octonauts_and_the_Ring_of_Fire","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Silvergate Media"},{"num":1055,"t":"Octonauts & the Great Barrier Reef","y":"2020","la":"4+","ca":"4+","full":"A musical Octonauts special built around a coral polyp named Corey who dreams of growing into a full coral, only to have his home reef threatened by a swarm of hungry, coral-eating starfish. It doubles as a gentle environmental-science lesson on real reef ecosystems, delivered through original songs. Sensitive younger viewers might feel a little worried watching the reef and its creatures put in danger, but the show never lingers on the threat and resolves it with a hopeful ending, modeling teamwork, kindness, and outreach to a scared new friend.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/octonauts-the-great-barrier-reef","addedAt":"2026-09-04","addedVia":"request","source":"EXTRA","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"Silvergate Media"}];
